#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""qb-doctor — is this host actually wired up, and can work land from it?

Three questions, one command, one report.

THE FIRST QUESTION (#204's original thesis). Three independently versioned things
have to agree — the board image, the harness on PATH, the Python client's venv —
and no component can compare them, because each one can only see itself. So each
one's staleness surfaces as a different one's error: `qb-dash` said "board
unreachable" about a board that was up (the image was six days old), `qb-board`
said "build a venv" about a venv that was fine (the checkout was 269 commits
behind), and the fix for that broke the MCP server. Four symptoms, not one of
which named its cause. The value here is not any single check; it is that the
version lines get compared to each other in one place.

THE SECOND QUESTION (the note added 2026-08-22). Is each of the harness's own
guards INSTALLED — not written, not tested, not documented, INSTALLED on this
machine? One morning produced four instances of the same failure: a mechanism
that shipped and was never wired up. The `qb-reconcile` timer existed in-repo and
on no host, so the plan went 39% stale. The `reference-transaction` stash guard
existed in `harness/githooks/` and in no repo, so an agent popped another
worktree's work. `HUMAN_EDGE_SECRET` was documented in DEPLOY.md with a checklist
and never deployed, so every human-only endpoint has 403'd since v2.39. Not one
announced itself; each was found by tripping over a downstream symptom, which is
the pattern this file's first paragraph is about.

THE THIRD QUESTION (#406, and the `landing` group #407 fills out). Can work
actually LAND from here? The first two are both about this machine, and on the
night #406 was filed they were both satisfied: 9 ok, 1 unknown, nothing broken.
In the same minute the merge queue held seven green pull requests with none
ready, main had not moved in three hours, `refs/tags/v3.8` pointed at a commit
that is not in main's history, and three branches were conflicting on the one
file `changelog.d/` exists to keep them out of. Every row was correct and not one
of them was about the pipeline the work has to travel down. There is a row for
each of those now — `queue`, `landed`, `tags`, `generated` — beside `merges`,
which asks whether a merge here can rewrite the commit a release tag was reserved
against, and `stamping`, which asks whether the affordance #122 removed is still
reachable from this host.

AND THE RULE THAT KEEPS THAT GROUP HONEST: most of it needs the board or GitHub,
and a landing group that went green having seen neither would be a worse version
of the problem it exists to solve. So every row that needs something it could not
reach says `unknown` and says which thing — and none of them fails a repo for not
using the mechanism it is about. An empty queue on a repo with no pull requests
is fine; a repo that does not do releases out of fragments needs no fragment row.
Nothing in the group merges, re-points a tag or evicts a queue entry: #405 argued
the queue must stay advisory and the argument held under measurement.

THE RULE THAT DECIDES THE SHAPE OF EVERY CHECK BELOW: look where the mechanism
RUNS, never where its source lives. `ls harness/githooks/reference-transaction`
succeeds on a host with no guard installed at all — the file is in git, which is
precisely what every one of the four had going for it. So the hooks check reads
`core.hooksPath` and the files under the common git dir; the reconcile check asks
systemd; the edge check makes a live request.

AND WHAT A ROW IS, since #408: a verdict, a written explanation of why it
matters, and a brief an agent can be handed without a person writing one. The
diagnosis half of this tool worked from the day it landed — it found four unwired
mechanisms in one morning and a fifth within three hours of its own release — and
every one of those findings then became a person reading a row, deciding what it
meant, and hand-writing a brief. That step is the bottleneck and it is the one
that cannot be scaled by adding agents. `CheckSpec` is where the explanation and
the brief live; `Brief.needs` is why a row that could not be evaluated produces no
brief instead of a confident one.

AND THE RULE THAT DECIDES HOW THEY REPORT: a check that could not be made is NOT
a check that passed. This repo has now settled that argument three times — #244
(`prune-worktrees` called a skipped scan "Clean"), `qb-reconcile`'s
could-not-check versus nothing-to-report, and #324's `qbdata.CI_STATES`, where an
absent CI result must not render as a benign one. A doctor that prints `ok`
because it could not look is worse than one that does not check, because it
launders ignorance into assurance — and unlike a stale layer, a false green never
announces itself later.

Exit codes, and they are the same distinction:

    0   every check ran; none failed (warnings are still a 0 — the host works)
    1   at least one check COULD NOT BE MADE, and none of the rest failed
    2   at least one check ran and failed
    3   could not run at all: bad arguments, or no repo to look at

Usage:
    qb-doctor                     every check against this host
    qb-doctor --fix               also run the installers that are safe to re-run
    qb-doctor --json              the whole report as JSON
    qb-doctor --repo DIR          check that checkout instead of the cwd's
    qb-doctor --only hooks,edge   just those rows
    qb-doctor --only landing      a whole group of them (host | landing)
    qb-doctor --human-url URL     the browser vhost, for the edge check
    qb-doctor --quiet             print only the rows that are not ok
    qb-doctor --explain           why each bad row matters, and its ready-made brief
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
import contextlib
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import textwrap
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from datetime import UTC, datetime
from pathlib import Path
from urllib.parse import quote

#: The four answers a check can give. Closed, and closed for the reason
#: `qbdata.CI_STATES` is closed (#324): these feed a count, a glyph and an exit
#: code, and a fifth value invented at a call site would leave the numerator
#: while still counting as coverage.
#:
#: They are four because collapsing any pair loses the distinction that bit:
#:
#: * ``ok``      — the check ran and the answer is good.
#: * ``warn``    — the check ran, the mechanism works, and it carries residue no
#:                 installer can clear. Installing the stash guard does not drain
#:                 the entries pushed before it, so a freshly-guarded repo is
#:                 protected going forward and still holds its old landmines.
#:                 ``guard active, 4 pre-guard entries remain`` is the report; a
#:                 bare ``ok`` is not.
#: * ``fail``    — the check ran and the answer is bad.
#: * ``unknown`` — the check could not be made, and why. NOT a synonym for ``ok``
#:                 and not a synonym for ``fail`` either: the remedy for a guard
#:                 that is off is to switch it on, and the remedy for a guard
#:                 nobody could ask about is to make it askable.
VERDICTS = ("ok", "warn", "fail", "unknown")

#: Which verdicts mean "this host is wired up". `warn` is in it on purpose: the
#: mechanism is installed and working, and the residue beside it is a separate
#: piece of work with its own remedy.
HEALTHY = ("ok", "warn")

_GLYPHS = {"ok": "ok", "warn": "warn", "fail": "FAIL", "unknown": "?"}

#: ANSI, applied only to a TTY and never when `NO_COLOR` is set. `unknown` shares
#: yellow with `warn` rather than borrowing green, because the one rendering this
#: file exists to forbid is the one where a check nobody could make looks fine.
_COLOURS = {"ok": "\033[0;32m", "warn": "\033[1;33m", "fail": "\033[0;31m",
            "unknown": "\033[1;33m"}
_RESET = "\033[0m"

#: Seconds for any single HTTP probe. Short: this is a diagnostic somebody is
#: waiting on, and "the board did not answer in five seconds" is itself a finding.
HTTP_TIMEOUT = 8

#: Seconds for one subprocess (`git`, `systemctl`, `gh`). `git fetch` is the slow
#: one and it is the reason this is not 5.
CMD_TIMEOUT = 30

#: Bytes read from any one response. Generous rather than tidy, because the
#: response this has to survive is `/openapi.json`, which is a megabyte of schema
#: on a board this size. A 64 KiB cap truncated it mid-object, `json.loads` raised,
#: and the board row reported "carried no info.version" about a document that
#: carried one — a version check answering `unknown` because of its own buffer,
#: which is this file's subject happening to this file.
MAX_BODY = 8 * 1024 * 1024

#: The runbook's file name. Only the name is a constant, because only the name is
#: stable: the file moves between `issues/open` and `issues/closed` as the issue's
#: state changes, and this used to hardcode `issues/open`.
#:
#: It moved to `closed` on 2026-08-22, when the secret was actually deployed — so
#: from that day the one row whose entire purpose is *"the remedy belongs to a
#: person, and a path is the remedy"* printed a path to nothing. A stale pointer is
#: worse here than no pointer: it sends somebody looking in a directory the file is
#: not in, for a document that does exist.
EDGE_RUNBOOK_FILE = "160-bug-quarterback-human-writes-refused-at-the-edge.md"

#: Both states the issue can be in, newest-first by likelihood: a runbook worth
#: naming is usually one whose problem has been solved at least once.
EDGE_RUNBOOK_DIRS = ("issues/closed", "issues/open")

#: The checkout it lives in. An env override because this is a path into ANOTHER
#: repository, which is the one thing this script cannot derive from its own.
SELFHOST_REPO_ENV = "SELFHOST_REPO"


def edge_runbook() -> str:
    """Where the runbook is on THIS box, or how to find it when it is not here.

    Resolved rather than asserted. The alternative — a constant naming one of the
    two directories — is what shipped, and it was wrong within a fortnight for a
    reason no reader could have predicted from the constant: the issue was closed,
    so the file moved, so the remedy pointed at nothing.

    Never returns a path that does not exist. A box without the selfhost checkout
    gets a sentence saying which repository to look in, which is a worse remedy
    than a path and a much better one than a path that is a lie.
    """
    root = Path(os.environ.get(SELFHOST_REPO_ENV) or Path.home() / "source" / "selfhost")
    for where in EDGE_RUNBOOK_DIRS:
        found = root / where / EDGE_RUNBOOK_FILE
        if found.is_file():
            # `~` rather than the absolute path, to match every other path this
            # tool prints and because the reader is the person whose home it is.
            try:
                return f"~/{found.relative_to(Path.home())}"
            except ValueError:
                return str(found)
    return (f"{EDGE_RUNBOOK_FILE} in the selfhost repo — not on this box "
            f"(set {SELFHOST_REPO_ENV} if it is checked out elsewhere)")


@dataclass
class Check:
    """One row of the report.

    ``fix`` and ``manual`` are the two halves of #204's third property, and they
    are separate fields rather than one command plus a flag so that nothing can
    run a command the author did not mark runnable. ``fix`` is an argv this tool
    will execute under ``--fix``: it must be idempotent, must need no secret, and
    must be safe to run against a host that is already correct. ``manual`` is a
    command STRING printed for a person and never executed — the edge secret
    needs 1Password and a Portainer redeploy, and a doctor that tried would fail
    halfway through somebody's production deploy.
    """

    name: str
    subject: str
    detail: str
    verdict: str
    manual: str | None = None
    fix: list[str] | None = None
    extra: dict = field(default_factory=dict)
    #: Which question this row answers. Assigned by `run_checks` from `CHECKS` rather
    #: than passed at every construction site, so a check function cannot file itself
    #: under a group its registration disagrees with.
    group: str = "host"
    #: Why this row matters, in prose — copied off the registration by `run_checks`,
    #: for the same reason `group` is. Per-ROW and not per-verdict: the paragraph is
    #: the same however the row came out, because it is about the mechanism rather
    #: than about today's answer.
    explanation: str = ""
    #: The brief, rendered — or None, which is a real and common answer. Assigned by
    #: `run_checks` from the registration's per-verdict `briefs`; see :func:`compose`.
    prompt: str | None = None
    #: Who the prompt above is for, in `AUDIENCES`. None when there is none.
    prompt_for: str | None = None
    #: Why there is no prompt, when there is none. Never empty in that case, because
    #: a row that silently carries no brief is indistinguishable from one whose brief
    #: could not be assembled, and those are opposite facts.
    no_prompt: str = ""

    def __post_init__(self) -> None:
        assert self.verdict in VERDICTS, f"{self.verdict!r} is not one of {VERDICTS}"

    def as_dict(self) -> dict:
        return {"name": self.name, "group": self.group, "subject": self.subject,
                "detail": self.detail, "verdict": self.verdict, "manual": self.manual,
                "fix": self.fix, "explanation": self.explanation, "prompt": self.prompt,
                "prompt_for": self.prompt_for, "no_prompt": self.no_prompt or None,
                **({"extra": self.extra} if self.extra else {})}


#: Who a brief is written for. Two, and they do not merge: an `agent` brief is a
#: piece of work with constraints and a finish line; a `human` brief is an
#: escalation — a judgement, a password, a deploy, something nothing here may
#: decide. #279's `needs_human` draws the same line one layer up.
AUDIENCES = ("agent", "human")


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Brief:
    """What to hand somebody when a row comes out one particular way.

    Written **at the same time as the check, by whoever understood the fault** —
    #408 is explicit that a brief generated from the error message afterwards
    restates the assertion, which is the one thing nobody needed written down. The
    fields are the shape every good brief in this repo's history already had, named
    so that a registration missing one is visibly missing it:

    * ``task``        — what to do, in the imperative. Templated against the row's
                        ``extra``, so the fixed half is the understanding and the
                        variable half is what today's run actually found.
    * ``constraints`` — what NOT to do, and what has already been settled. This is
                        the half that stops an agent redesigning something on its
                        own authority, and it is the half a generated prompt can
                        never contain.
    * ``verify``      — how to know it worked. Never "the command exited 0": #414
                        is an entire issue about an installer's exit code being read
                        as the guard being installed, and the answer there was to
                        re-check. :func:`compose` therefore appends the re-check to
                        every agent brief itself.
    * ``needs``       — the ``extra`` keys the task is written around. An evidence
                        gate, not documentation: a brief whose facts the row did not
                        report is not rendered at all.

    ``manual`` on the `Check` is NOT this, and the two must not merge. A `manual` is
    one command line a person runs; a brief is a paragraph nobody executes. Collapse
    them and you either put an unrunnable paragraph where the report prints a
    command, or lose the command.
    """

    audience: str
    task: str
    constraints: tuple[str, ...] = ()
    verify: tuple[str, ...] = ()
    needs: tuple[str, ...] = ()

    def __post_init__(self) -> None:
        assert self.audience in AUDIENCES, f"{self.audience!r} is not one of {AUDIENCES}"
        assert self.task.strip(), "a brief with no task is not a brief"


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class CheckSpec:
    """One registration: the check, and everything about it that is not a verdict.

    This used to be `(name, group, fn)`, and #408 is what stopped it being a tuple —
    a six-tuple carrying prose is unreadable at the call site and unaddressable
    everywhere else. The property the tuple had is kept: `--only` still validates a
    name against this registry before anything runs.

    ``explanation`` and ``briefs`` live HERE rather than on the `Check` for the
    reason `group` does. The understanding is written once, beside the predicate,
    by the person who had it; a check function that could compose its own brief
    could compose one its registration disagrees with, and the docstring — where
    this understanding lives today — is a place nothing can reach.

    ``briefs`` is keyed by verdict, and a verdict that is not a key gets **no
    brief**. That is the default and it is the safe one: most rows have nothing
    useful to say about their own `ok`, and an `unknown` splits in two — "this host
    cannot see it" is a person's job and "this repo is in a shape nothing here can
    parse" is an agent's, and only the registration knows which.
    """

    name: str
    group: str
    fn: object
    explanation: str
    briefs: dict = field(default_factory=dict)

    def __post_init__(self) -> None:
        assert self.explanation.strip(), f"{self.name} has no explanation"
        for verdict, brief in self.briefs.items():
            assert verdict in VERDICTS, f"{self.name}: {verdict!r} is not a verdict"
            assert isinstance(brief, Brief), f"{self.name}: {verdict} is not a Brief"


#: How systemd says it could not be asked. Matched FIRST, before anything is read as
#: an answer about a unit, because "No such file or directory" is systemd's wording
#: for a missing unit file AND for a missing bus socket — so a rule that looked for
#: that phrase alone called a dead session bus an uninstalled timer. Codex found that
#: on the first fix; the tail of the message is ambiguous, the head is not.
_BUS_UNREACHABLE = ("failed to connect to bus", "no medium found",
                    "operation not permitted", "connection refused")

#: How systemd says the unit is not there, when it says it on stderr rather than
#: answering `not-found` on stdout. Requires the "unit file" wording, so it cannot
#: match a bus error that happens to end the same way.
_MISSING_UNIT = ("unit file state", "no such unit", "unit file", "does not exist")


def clip_line(text: str, width: int = 90) -> str:
    """The first line of a tool's stderr, short enough to sit in a report row."""
    first = (text or "").strip().splitlines()
    if not first:
        return ""
    return first[0] if len(first[0]) <= width else first[0][:width - 1] + "…"


def run_cmd(argv: list[str], *, cwd: str | None = None, env: dict | None = None,
            timeout: int = CMD_TIMEOUT, stdin: str | None = None) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
    """One subprocess. Returns ``(rc, stdout, stderr)``; never raises.

    ``stdin`` exists for the one caller that has to use it: a model CLI is handed a
    prompt carrying every document it may read, and Linux caps a SINGLE argv entry
    at 128 KiB — so the obvious positional form fails with `E2BIG` on a corpus of
    any size, and fails as an OSError rather than as an answer.

    A missing binary comes back as ``(127, "", "<name>: not found")`` rather than
    an exception, because "this host has no systemctl" is an answer a check has
    to be able to turn into an `unknown` — and an exception at the call site
    would have to be turned back into one anyway, in each of the six places.
    """
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=cwd, env=env, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=timeout, input=stdin)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return 127, "", f"{argv[0]}: not found"
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, "", f"{argv[0]}: timed out after {timeout}s"
    except OSError as exc:                                    # noqa: BLE001 — report it
        return 126, "", f"{argv[0]}: {exc}"
    return p.returncode, p.stdout.strip(), p.stderr.strip()


def http_get(url: str, headers: dict | None = None) -> tuple[int, str, str | None]:
    """One GET. Returns ``(status, body, error)``; ``status`` is 0 when there was none.

    Redirects are NOT followed, and that is load-bearing for the edge check: a
    302 to a forward-auth portal is the answer ("this host has no session"), and
    following it would turn it into a 200 carrying a login page, which reads as
    the board answering.
    """

    class _NoRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):
        def redirect_request(self, *_args, **_kw):            # noqa: D102
            return None

    opener = urllib.request.build_opener(_NoRedirect)
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers=headers or {})
    try:
        with opener.open(req, timeout=HTTP_TIMEOUT) as resp:
            return resp.status, resp.read(MAX_BODY).decode("utf-8", "replace"), None
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:
        body = ""
        with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
            body = exc.read(MAX_BODY).decode("utf-8", "replace")
        return exc.code, body, None
    except Exception as exc:                                  # noqa: BLE001
        return 0, "", f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}"


@dataclass
class Host:
    """Everything the checks need to know about where they are looking.

    Assembled once by :func:`survey` and passed in, so every check function is a
    pure-ish function of it and a test can drive one without a board, a git repo
    or a systemd.
    """

    repo: Path
    common_git_dir: Path | None
    base_url: str | None
    token: str | None
    human_url: str | None
    harness_bin: Path | None
    source_harness: Path
    githooks: Path | None
    #: The checkout whose `mcp/.venv` this host's MCP shim actually runs, which is
    #: `QUARTERBACK_REPO` and NOT necessarily `repo`. They differ every time this is
    #: pointed at a worktree with `--repo`, and the honest answer there is still the
    #: configured one: `qb-mcp` execs `$QUARTERBACK_REPO/mcp/.venv/bin/python` whatever
    #: directory you happen to be standing in. Resolved in `survey` rather than read
    #: from the environment inside the check, so a test can set it and a reader can
    #: see that every check is a function of this object.
    client_repo: Path | None = None
    remote: str = "origin"
    #: One `gh pr list` answer, shared by the rows that need it. Two rows asked for
    #: overlapping fields and paid for two round trips to say different things about the
    #: same pull requests, which is both slow and a way for them to disagree. Per-Host
    #: rather than module-level so a test drives one survey and not a leftover.
    gh_cache: dict = field(default_factory=dict)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# survey — resolve the host once
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def source_harness_dir(script: str) -> Path:
    """The `harness/` tree this script belongs to.

    ``readlink -f`` deliberately, unlike `qb-mcp`'s one-link-at-a-time walk: this
    file is only ever run out of a checkout or out of the package's own `bin/`,
    both of which keep `bin/` and `githooks/` as siblings, and neither of which
    home-manager flattens the way it flattens the board client's four.
    """
    return Path(os.path.realpath(script)).parent.parent


def githooks_dir(harness: Path) -> Path | None:
    """Where `qb-hooks` reads its hook sources from — the two layouts it knows.

    Read from the same two places rather than hard-coded, because this is what
    the hooks check compares the INSTALLED set against. Hard-coding the file
    names here would mean #343's `pre-push` landing in `githooks/` and this tool
    reporting a repo with no `pre-push` as fully hooked.
    """
    for cand in (os.environ.get("QB_GITHOOKS_DIR"),
                 harness / "githooks",
                 harness / "share" / "quarterback-harness" / "githooks"):
        if cand and Path(cand, "reference-transaction").is_file():
            return Path(cand)
    return None


def installed_harness_bin() -> Path | None:
    """The `bin/` of the harness that is actually on PATH, or None.

    Resolved through `create-worktree` — the package's `mainProgram`, and a name
    no checkout puts on PATH by accident — rather than through `qb-doctor`
    itself, which is the one script guaranteed absent from PATH on the branch
    that adds it.
    """
    found = shutil.which("create-worktree")
    return Path(os.path.realpath(found)).parent if found else None


def load_site_config() -> dict:
    """`qb-env`'s config file, parsed as the shell assignments it is.

    Parsed rather than sourced. Sourcing it would run whatever is in it as this
    user, and the file is machine-generated by home-manager on some hosts and
    hand-written on others; a diagnostic must not be the thing that executes an
    unreviewed line. `QUARTERBACK_TOKEN_CMD` is a command and is left as text —
    :func:`resolve_token` is where the decision to run it is made and stated.
    """
    path = Path(os.environ.get(
        "QUARTERBACK_CONFIG",
        Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME", Path.home() / ".config")) / "quarterback/config"))
    out: dict = {}
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except OSError:
        return out
    for line in text.splitlines():
        m = re.match(r"^\s*(?:export\s+)?([A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*)=(.*)$", line)
        if not m:
            continue
        value = m.group(2).strip()
        if len(value) >= 2 and value[0] == value[-1] and value[0] in "\"'":
            value = value[1:-1]
        out[m.group(1)] = value
    return out


def resolve_token(cfg: dict) -> str | None:
    """This machine's bearer, by `qb-env`'s own precedence — env, TOKEN_CMD, tmpfs.

    The TOKEN_CMD branch runs a command out of the config file, which
    :func:`load_site_config` refuses to do wholesale. The narrowing is the point:
    that one variable is DEFINED as a command to run (`qb-env` calls it "the
    per-site hook … kept cheap — it runs on every call"), so running it is using
    the file as documented rather than executing it.
    """
    if os.environ.get("QUARTERBACK_TOKEN"):
        return os.environ["QUARTERBACK_TOKEN"]
    cmd = cfg.get("QUARTERBACK_TOKEN_CMD")
    if cmd:
        # `QUARTERBACK_AGENT` is exported before the command runs because every
        # site's TOKEN_CMD interpolates it — `op read op://…/quarterback-$AGENT/token`
        # on this fleet — and `qb-env` sets it from the hostname before resolving.
        # Without it the command reads a path with an empty segment and returns
        # nothing, which this would report as "no token on this machine".
        env = {**os.environ, "QUARTERBACK_AGENT": cfg.get("QUARTERBACK_AGENT") or agent_name()}
        rc, out, _ = run_cmd(["bash", "-c", cmd], env=env, timeout=15)
        if rc == 0 and out.splitlines():
            return out.splitlines()[0].strip() or None
    try:
        return (Path("/run/op-secrets/quarterback-token").read_text().strip() or None)
    except OSError:
        return None


def agent_name() -> str:
    """This machine's name in the token file, `qb-env`'s default: the short hostname."""
    import socket
    return socket.gethostname().split(".", 1)[0]


def survey(repo_arg: str | None, human_url_arg: str | None, script: str) -> Host:
    """Resolve everything the checks look at. Raises ``SystemExit(3)`` with no repo."""
    start = Path(repo_arg).expanduser() if repo_arg else Path.cwd()
    rc, top, _ = run_cmd(["git", "-C", str(start), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if rc != 0:
        print(f"qb-doctor: {start} is not a git repository — there is nothing to check "
              "against. Run it inside a quarterback checkout, or pass --repo.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        raise SystemExit(3)
    repo = Path(top)
    rc, common, _ = run_cmd(
        ["git", "-C", str(repo), "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"])
    cfg = load_site_config()
    harness = source_harness_dir(script)
    return Host(
        repo=repo,
        common_git_dir=Path(common) if rc == 0 and common else None,
        base_url=os.environ.get("QUARTERBACK_BASE_URL") or cfg.get("QUARTERBACK_BASE_URL"),
        token=resolve_token(cfg),
        human_url=(human_url_arg or os.environ.get("QUARTERBACK_HUMAN_URL")
                   or cfg.get("QUARTERBACK_HUMAN_URL")),
        harness_bin=installed_harness_bin(),
        source_harness=harness,
        githooks=githooks_dir(harness),
        client_repo=_as_path(os.environ.get("QUARTERBACK_REPO")
                             or cfg.get("QUARTERBACK_REPO")) or repo,
    )


def _as_path(value: str | None) -> Path | None:
    """A config value that names a directory, resolved the way a shell would.

    `$HOME` and `~` both, because the config file is shell and this fleet's
    generated one writes `QUARTERBACK_REPO="$HOME/source/quarterback"`. Reading
    that literally put the client row at a path with a dollar sign in it and
    reported `no MCP venv` about a venv that was there — a check answering
    confidently about the wrong place, which is worse than not checking.
    `expandvars` substitutes; it does not execute, so this stays inside the rule
    that the config is parsed rather than sourced.
    """
    if not value:
        return None
    return Path(os.path.expanduser(os.path.expandvars(value)))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# the three version lines (#204's original thesis)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def declared_version(repo: Path) -> str | None:
    """The version `app/main.py` declares — what a board built from here would serve.

    Read out of the source text rather than by importing the app: this runs on
    hosts with no venv and no database, and importing `app.main` needs both.
    """
    try:
        text = (repo / "app" / "main.py").read_text()
    except OSError:
        return None
    m = re.search(r"""FastAPI\([^)]*version\s*=\s*["']([^"']+)["']""", text, re.S)
    return m.group(1) if m else None


def _version_tuple(v: str) -> tuple:
    return tuple(int(p) if p.isdigit() else 0 for p in v.split("."))


def check_board(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Is the running board built from something at least as new as this checkout?

    THE VERSION COMES FROM `/openapi.json`, and that is not a workaround. CHANGELOG.md
    opens by defining the board's version as exactly this — "what `GET /openapi.json`
    reports in `.info.version` — the way to tell which release a running instance is
    on" — and it is the same string `app/main.py` declares, so the two sides of this
    comparison are the same fact measured in two places. #199 asks for a first-class
    `/version` and should still land; it would make this cheaper, not truer.

    Capability probing (`/review/needs-human` present means >= v2.72) is the fallback
    below and is deliberately second. It answers with a RANGE, and a range is not a
    version: it can say the board is new enough for one feature while telling you
    nothing about the next one. So a board that will not serve its own metadata is
    reported as `unknown` with the range as context, never as a pass.
    """
    if not host.base_url:
        return Check("board", "-", "QUARTERBACK_BASE_URL is unset and there is no default",
                     "unknown", manual="set QUARTERBACK_BASE_URL in ~/.config/quarterback/config")
    mine = declared_version(host.repo)
    status, body, err = http_get(f"{host.base_url}/openapi.json")
    if err or status != 200:
        return _board_by_capability(host, mine, err or f"HTTP {status}")
    try:
        served = json.loads(body).get("info", {}).get("version")
    except ValueError:
        served = None
    if not served:
        return _board_by_capability(host, mine, "/openapi.json carried no info.version")
    if not mine:
        return Check("board", host.base_url, f"serving {served}; this checkout declares no "
                     "version in app/main.py, so there is nothing to compare it to", "unknown",
                     extra={"served": served})
    return _board_verdict(host, served, mine)


def _board_verdict(host: Host, served: str, mine: str) -> Check:
    """The three-way compare, once both numbers are in hand."""
    extra = {"served": served, "declared": mine}
    if served == mine:
        return Check("board", host.base_url, f"{served}, matching this checkout", "ok",
                     extra=extra)
    if _version_tuple(served) < _version_tuple(mine):
        return Check(
            "board", host.base_url,
            f"serving {served}; this checkout declares {mine} — the image is behind",
            "fail",
            manual="rebuild and redeploy the board image (DEPLOY.md), then re-run",
            extra=extra)
    return Check("board", host.base_url,
                 f"serving {served}; this checkout declares {mine} — the CHECKOUT is behind",
                 "warn", manual=f"git -C {host.repo} pull", extra=extra)


#: Capability probes, newest first: a path, the method to try, the response that
#: proves the route exists, and the release that added it. Used only when the
#: board will not serve `/openapi.json`. A 405 counts as proof for a POST-only
#: route — the router matched the path and refused the verb, which is exactly the
#: evidence wanted and the reason a bare 404/200 test is not enough.
_CAPABILITIES = (
    ("/review-queue", "GET", (405,), "2.75"),
    ("/review/needs-human", "GET", (200, 401, 403, 422), "2.72"),
)


def _board_by_capability(host: Host, mine: str | None, why: str) -> Check:
    """A floor for the board's version, when it will not state one. Never a pass.

    Reported as `unknown` even when a probe succeeds, and the docstring on
    :func:`check_board` says why: this establishes ">= 2.75", and ">= 2.75" does
    not answer "does the board agree with this checkout".
    """
    floor = None
    for path, _method, proofs, release in _CAPABILITIES:
        headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {host.token}"} if host.token else {}
        status, _body, err = http_get(f"{host.base_url}{path}", headers)
        if err:
            break
        if status in proofs:
            floor = release
            break
    if floor:
        detail = (f"no version from the board ({why}); capability probing puts it at "
                  f">= {floor}, which is a floor and not a version"
                  + (f" — this checkout declares {mine}" if mine else ""))
    else:
        detail = f"no version from the board ({why}), and no capability probe answered either"
    return Check("board", host.base_url or "-", detail, "unknown",
                 manual="check the board is up and serving /openapi.json (#199 would make "
                        "this a first-class /version)",
                 extra={"floor": floor, "declared": mine})


def checkout_harness_bin(host: Host) -> Path | None:
    """The `bin/` this comparison is ABOUT: the one in the checkout under inspection.

    Found by Codex, and it is the finding that mattered most on this change. The
    first cut compared the harness on PATH against *this script's own* harness —
    which is the same directory whenever `qb-doctor` is run from PATH, i.e. in
    every installed use there will ever be. The row reported `ok` by comparing an
    installed harness with itself, forever, on the one check whose entire subject
    is a stale install. A version check that cannot go red is the false green this
    file opens by arguing against.

    So the checkout wins, and the script's own tree is only the fallback for a
    `--repo` that carries no `harness/` at all (`qb-doctor` pointed at some other
    project, where the question is still "is the harness on PATH the one I have").
    """
    from_repo = host.repo / "harness" / "bin"
    if from_repo.is_dir():
        return from_repo
    own = host.source_harness / "bin"
    return own if own.is_dir() else None


def check_harness(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Is the harness on PATH built from this checkout?

    The nix flake pin is what versions it, no harness script can tell you it is
    stale because each one only knows its own store path, and until #267 nothing
    acted on the answer either. So this compares CONTENT: every file the package
    installs from `bin/`, against the checkout's. That answers the question the
    version number would have, without needing one to exist.

    `qb-bump` is what acts on it now, and it reads this row rather than measuring
    anything itself — so this stays the one comparison, and the remedy below names
    a command instead of a chore.

    CONTENT IS A PROXY, and the next reader should know it is one. The question
    actually being asked is "was the harness on PATH built from a commit at or
    after this checkout's HEAD", and the truthful answer to that is in the flake
    pin's rev, not in the files. Reading the pin means finding the flake that
    CONSUMES this harness, which this tool cannot do — it knows the checkout and
    the store path, and the consuming flake is a third thing that lives somewhere
    else on some hosts and nowhere at all on others. So content stands in, and it
    is a proxy in both directions: it cannot tell an install that is AHEAD of this
    branch from one that is behind (hence `_harness_drift`'s one direction), and
    it counts anything the packaging rewrites unless the rewrite is undone here,
    which is what `_same_after_packaging` exists to do (#353).
    """
    if host.harness_bin is None:
        return Check("harness", "-", "no harness on PATH (create-worktree not found), so "
                     "nothing to compare this checkout against", "unknown",
                     manual="install the harness (harness/README.md, 'Installing')")
    src = checkout_harness_bin(host)
    if src is None:
        return Check("harness", str(host.harness_bin),
                     f"no harness/bin to compare against, in {host.repo} or beside this script",
                     "unknown")
    if host.harness_bin.resolve() == src.resolve():
        return Check("harness", str(host.harness_bin),
                     "the harness on PATH IS this checkout", "ok")
    differ, missing = _harness_drift(src, host.harness_bin)
    if not differ and not missing:
        return Check("harness", str(host.harness_bin),
                     f"identical to {src} ({len(list(src.iterdir()))} files)", "ok")
    parts = []
    if missing:
        parts.append(f"{len(missing)} absent ({_named(missing)})")
    if differ:
        parts.append(f"{len(differ)} differ ({_named(differ)})")
    return Check("harness", str(host.harness_bin),
                 "behind this checkout: " + ", ".join(parts), "fail",
                 manual="run `qb-bump`: it prepares that flake input's bump, builds this "
                        "machine's system with it, and hands you the one command that "
                        "needs your password (#267)",
                 extra={"differ": sorted(differ), "missing": sorted(missing)})


#: How many names a drift list spells out before it elides the rest. The row is
#: one line and `harness/bin` is around 30 files, so a cap has to exist; what may
#: not happen is applying one silently.
NAME_CAP = 4


def _named(names: set[str]) -> str:
    """The names, capped, with any elision SAID rather than left to be noticed (#358).

    The count beside this list is the full length, so a list that quietly stops at
    the cap contradicts the number in front of it — and because the count is the
    honest half, a reader who counts the names concludes the count is broken and
    stops trusting the row. `+N more` is the shape `qb-dash` already uses when it
    trims a table.
    """
    shown = sorted(names)[:NAME_CAP]
    rest = len(names) - len(shown)
    return ", ".join(shown) + (f", +{rest} more" if rest else "")


def _harness_drift(src: Path, installed: Path) -> tuple[set[str], set[str]]:
    """``(differing names, names absent from the install)``.

    One direction only. A file the INSTALL has and the checkout does not is a
    harness newer than this branch, which is normal on any branch that has not
    added a script, and reporting it would make the row red for every developer.
    """
    differ: set[str] = set()
    missing: set[str] = set()
    for f in src.iterdir():
        if not f.is_file():
            continue
        there = installed / f.name
        if not there.is_file():
            missing.add(f.name)
        elif not _same_after_packaging(f, there):
            differ.add(f.name)
    return differ, missing


def _wrapped_file(installed: Path, data: bytes) -> Path | None:
    """The file a `wrapProgram` wrapper wraps, or None if this is not a wrapper.

    `postInstall` wraps `qb-dash` to carry the dashboard's interpreter, and a
    wrapped program is no longer its source: nix renames the script to
    `.<name>-wrapped` and puts a generated shell script at the original name,
    whose job is to set a variable and `exec` the renamed one. It shares no bytes
    with the checkout's copy and never will, so byte-comparing it reports a file
    that is exactly right as drift, permanently.

    Detected structurally rather than by name — `postInstall` may wrap others,
    and a check that knows one filename would go quietly wrong on the second.
    Both halves are required: the sibling `.<name>-wrapped` has to exist AND the
    file at `name` has to point at it, which is what makes this a wrapper rather
    than a dotfile that happens to be lying there.

    Pointing at it means carrying its ABSOLUTE path, not its name. A bare name is
    a substring anything could hold — a comment, a doc string, an error message —
    and this function decides which file the caller reads instead, so a loose
    match here is a way for real drift to go unreported. The absolute path is not
    forgeable in the same way: it contains the build's own output hash, so no file
    written before the build can carry it. What nix generates is that full path,
    in both wrapper flavours — looked for in bytes rather than as text so a
    `makeBinaryWrapper` build, which compiles the same indirection into an ELF,
    reads the same way, and in two spellings so a `bin/` reached through a symlink
    still matches the store path the wrapper was written with.
    """
    wrapped = installed.with_name(f".{installed.name}-wrapped")
    if not wrapped.is_file():
        return None
    return wrapped if any(str(p).encode() in data
                          for p in (wrapped, wrapped.resolve())) else None


def _same_but_for_shebang(here: bytes, there: bytes) -> bool:
    """Equal, or equal once the first line of each is dropped.

    `package.nix`'s `postFixup` runs `patchShebangs`, deliberately and with its
    reason written down: an installed harness must not depend on what happens to
    be on the user's PATH. So every installed script differs from its source by
    its first line, for as long as that decision stands, and a comparison that
    counts it reported 24 files as behind the checkout on a host that had just
    rebuilt from it.

    The line is dropped only when the rest matches byte for byte, and only when
    BOTH files have one. `patchShebangs` rewrites a shebang; it never adds one and
    never removes one, so a file that gained or lost its first line changed by
    something other than the packaging.
    """
    if here == there:
        return True
    if not here.startswith(b"#!") or not there.startswith(b"#!"):
        return False
    return here.partition(b"\n")[2] == there.partition(b"\n")[2]


def _same_after_packaging(source: Path, installed: Path) -> bool:
    """Is the installed file this checkout's file, once the packaging is undone?

    Content first and the wrapper only as an explanation for a mismatch: a file
    that already matches is never read through the indirection, so the one step
    here that can pick a DIFFERENT file to compare is reached only for a file that
    does not match as it stands.
    """
    here = source.read_bytes()
    there = installed.read_bytes()
    if _same_but_for_shebang(here, there):
        return True
    wrapped = _wrapped_file(installed, there)
    return wrapped is not None and _same_but_for_shebang(here, wrapped.read_bytes())


def check_client(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Is the MCP client's venv there, importable, and pointing at THIS checkout?

    Both halves, because #203 and #200 are different failures wearing one face.
    A venv can be perfectly healthy and editable-installed from a checkout that
    has since drifted (#203), and the documented repair for that installs a
    different half of the package and breaks the server (#200). "Importable" on
    its own says nothing about which source tree answered.
    """
    repo = host.client_repo or host.repo
    py = repo / "mcp" / ".venv" / "bin" / "python"
    if not py.is_file() or not os.access(py, os.X_OK):
        return Check("client", str(py), "no MCP venv", "fail",
                     manual=f"cd {repo}/mcp && uv venv --python 3.12 .venv && uv pip install -e .")
    rc, out, err = run_cmd([
        str(py), "-c",
        "import mcp_server.server, mcp_server.board, mcp_server as m; print(m.__file__)"])
    if rc != 0:
        return Check("client", str(py), f"venv present but the modules will not import: "
                     f"{(err or out).splitlines()[-1] if (err or out) else f'exit {rc}'}", "fail",
                     manual=f"cd {repo}/mcp && uv pip install -e .")
    served_from = Path(out.strip()).resolve().parent.parent if out.strip() else None
    want = (repo / "mcp").resolve()
    if served_from and served_from != want:
        return Check("client", str(py),
                     f"imports fine, but from {served_from}, not {want} — the editable "
                     "install points at another checkout", "fail",
                     manual=f"cd {repo}/mcp && uv pip install -e .",
                     extra={"serving_from": str(served_from)})
    return Check("client", str(py), f"importable, from {want}", "ok")


def check_checkout(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Is this checkout current with its remote?

    `git status` alone cannot answer it — a branch is "up to date with
    origin/main" against whatever was last fetched, which on a box that has not
    fetched for a week is a sentence about the past. So this fetches first, and
    a fetch that fails is an `unknown` rather than a clean bill.
    """
    r = str(host.repo)
    rc, _out, err = run_cmd(["git", "-C", r, "fetch", "--quiet", host.remote])
    if rc != 0:
        return Check("checkout", r, f"could not fetch {host.remote}: {err or f'exit {rc}'}",
                     "unknown", manual=f"git -C {r} fetch {host.remote}")
    _rc, branch, _ = run_cmd(["git", "-C", r, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"])
    _rc, head, _ = run_cmd(["git", "-C", r, "rev-parse", "--short", "HEAD"])
    base = _default_branch(host)
    if base is None:
        return Check("checkout", r, f"{branch} @ {head}; could not resolve "
                     f"{host.remote}'s default branch", "unknown")
    rc3, count, _ = run_cmd(
        ["git", "-C", r, "rev-list", "--count", f"HEAD..{host.remote}/{base}"])
    subject = f"{branch} @ {head}"
    if rc3 != 0 or not count.isdigit():
        return Check("checkout", r, f"{subject}; could not count against "
                     f"{host.remote}/{base}", "unknown")
    behind = int(count)
    if behind == 0:
        return Check("checkout", r, f"{subject}, 0 behind {host.remote}/{base}", "ok")
    return Check("checkout", r,
                 f"{subject}, {behind} behind {host.remote}/{base}", "warn",
                 manual=f"git -C {r} pull", extra={"behind": behind})


def _default_branch(host: Host) -> str | None:
    """The remote's default branch, from its own HEAD ref."""
    rc, out, _ = run_cmd(["git", "-C", str(host.repo), "symbolic-ref", "--short",
                          f"refs/remotes/{host.remote}/HEAD"])
    if rc == 0 and "/" in out:
        return out.split("/", 1)[1]
    for guess in ("main", "master"):
        rc, _, _ = run_cmd(["git", "-C", str(host.repo), "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet",
                            f"refs/remotes/{host.remote}/{guess}"])
        if rc == 0:
            return guess
    return None


def check_token(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Can this machine actually authenticate to its board?

    #201's defect: `qb-board` said "this machine has no token" while the token
    was valid — the client never set the variable its own token command needs.
    The only answer that settles it is a live authenticated call, so this makes
    one rather than checking that a variable is non-empty.
    """
    if not host.base_url:
        return Check("token", "-", "no board configured (QUARTERBACK_BASE_URL unset)", "fail",
                     manual="set QUARTERBACK_BASE_URL in ~/.config/quarterback/config")
    if not host.token:
        return Check("token", host.base_url, "no token resolved (QUARTERBACK_TOKEN, "
                     "QUARTERBACK_TOKEN_CMD, /run/op-secrets/quarterback-token all empty)",
                     "fail", manual="set QUARTERBACK_TOKEN_CMD in ~/.config/quarterback/config")
    status, body, err = http_get(f"{host.base_url}/whoami",
                                 {"Authorization": f"Bearer {host.token}"})
    if err:
        return Check("token", host.base_url, f"could not reach the board: {err}", "unknown")
    if status == 401:
        return Check("token", host.base_url, "the board refused this machine's bearer (401)",
                     "fail", manual="re-mint the token (QUARTERBACK_TOKEN_REFRESH_CMD) and "
                                    "check API_TOKENS on the board")
    if status != 200:
        return Check("token", host.base_url, f"/whoami answered {status}", "unknown")
    try:
        who = json.loads(body)
    except ValueError:
        return Check("token", host.base_url, "/whoami did not answer JSON", "unknown")
    return Check("token", host.base_url,
                 f"resolved — this session is {who.get('agent')}", "ok",
                 extra={"agent": who.get("agent"), "kind": who.get("kind")})


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# the guards (the 2026-08-22 note)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def expected_hooks(host: Host) -> set[str] | None:
    """Every file `qb-hooks` has to put in a repo's hook dir, from the SOURCES.

    Derived, never listed. #343 is adding a `pre-push` guard in another worktree
    right now, and a hard-coded pair here would report a repo with no `pre-push`
    as fully hooked from the day that lands — which is this file's whole subject
    happening to this file.
    """
    if host.githooks is None:
        return None
    return {f.name for f in host.githooks.iterdir() if f.is_file()}


def check_hooks(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Is the stash guard INSTALLED in this repo — not present in git, installed?

    `ls harness/githooks/reference-transaction` succeeds on a host with no guard
    at all. What makes a guard run is `core.hooksPath` pointing at a directory
    that holds it, so that is what this reads.
    """
    r = str(host.repo)
    if host.common_git_dir is None:
        return Check("hooks", r, "could not resolve the common git dir", "unknown")
    want_dir = host.common_git_dir / "qb-hooks"
    rc, current, _ = run_cmd(["git", "-C", r, "config", "--type=path", "--get", "core.hooksPath"])
    fix = ["qb-hooks", "install", "--repo", r]
    if rc != 0 or not current:
        return Check("hooks", str(want_dir), "core.hooksPath is unset — no guard runs here",
                     "fail", manual=f"qb-hooks install --repo {r}", fix=fix)
    if Path(current).resolve() != want_dir.resolve():
        return Check("hooks", current, f"core.hooksPath points elsewhere (expected {want_dir})",
                     "fail", manual=f"qb-hooks install --repo {r}", fix=fix)
    want = expected_hooks(host)
    if want is None:
        return Check("hooks", current, "installed, but this harness has no githooks/ sources "
                     "to compare against, so the file list cannot be checked", "unknown",
                     manual="reinstall the harness — githooks/ is missing from it")
    here = Path(current)
    absent = sorted(n for n in want
                    if not (here / n).is_file() or not os.access(here / n, os.X_OK))
    if absent:
        return Check("hooks", current,
                     f"installed, but missing {len(absent)} of {len(want)}: {', '.join(absent)}",
                     "fail", manual=f"qb-hooks install --repo {r}", fix=fix,
                     extra={"missing": absent})
    return Check("hooks", current, f"installed ({', '.join(sorted(want))})", "ok",
                 extra={"installed": sorted(want)})


def check_stash(host: Host) -> Check:
    """The SHARED `refs/stash`, and the residue installing the guard cannot clear.

    Every worktree of a repo shares one `refs/stash` — it is in the common git
    dir — so an entry pushed from any of them is `stash@{0}` for all of them.
    #210's hook refuses new entries and deliberately allows deletions, so
    installing it protects a repo going forward and drains nothing: a
    freshly-guarded repo is exactly as mined as it was an hour earlier. `ok` for
    that state would be a lie of omission, so it is a `warn` naming the count,
    which is the behaviour `qb-hooks status` already models and the only reason
    the four entries on this repo were ever found.
    """
    rc, out, err = run_cmd(["git", "-C", str(host.repo), "stash", "list"])
    if rc != 0:
        return Check("stash", "refs/stash", f"could not list: {err or f'exit {rc}'}", "unknown")
    n = len([ln for ln in out.splitlines() if ln.strip()])
    guarded = check_hooks(host).verdict == "ok"
    if n == 0:
        # Clean AND unguarded is still not `ok`. The ref is empty because nobody
        # has stashed yet, not because anything stops them — which is the state
        # this repo was in for weeks before an agent popped another worktree's
        # work out of it. The remedy points at the hooks row rather than
        # repeating its command, so `--fix` reports one installation and not two.
        return Check("stash", "refs/stash",
                     "0 entries" + (" (guard active)" if guarded
                                    else ", but nothing guards the shared ref"),
                     "ok" if guarded else "warn",
                     manual=None if guarded else "see the hooks row — the guard is not installed")
    word = "entry" if n == 1 else "entries"
    if guarded:
        return Check("stash", "refs/stash",
                     f"guard active, {n} pre-guard {word} remain — the hook allows deletions "
                     "so nothing drains them for you", "warn",
                     manual=f"git -C {host.repo} stash list   # then drop each, once you know "
                            "whose it is", extra={"entries": n})
    return Check("stash", "refs/stash",
                 f"{n} {word} on the shared ref and NO guard installed", "fail",
                 manual="see the hooks row — install the guard, then drop the entries above",
                 extra={"entries": n})


def unit_state(verb: str, unit: str) -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """``(what systemctl said, None)`` — or ``("", why it could not say)``.

    One place, for both `is-enabled` and `is-active`, because every caller has to
    make the same three-way distinction and a second hand-rolled copy is how the
    two come to disagree about which failures are answers.

    `systemctl` exits non-zero for perfectly good answers — 4 for `not-found`, 1 for
    `disabled` — so the exit code cannot be the discriminator. What settles it is
    whether anything was SAID: a word on stdout is an answer whatever the code, an
    empty stdout with a bus error is not an answer at all, and an empty stdout with
    systemd's unit-file wording is the answer `not-found`.
    """
    rc, out, err = run_cmd(["systemctl", "--user", verb, unit])
    if out:
        return out, None
    low = (err or "").lower()
    if any(phrase in low for phrase in _BUS_UNREACHABLE):
        return "", clip_line(err) or f"{verb}: exit {rc}"
    if any(phrase in low for phrase in _MISSING_UNIT):
        return "not-found", None
    if rc != 0:
        return "", clip_line(err) or f"{verb}: exit {rc}"
    return "", f"{verb} said nothing"


def check_reconcile(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Is the reconcile timer enabled and running ON THIS HOST?

    It shipped with its units in `harness/loops/systemd/` and was installed on no
    host at all, so the plan `plan_read` hands a cold agent went 39% wrong — 13 of
    33 items pointing at closed issues — while every component involved reported
    itself healthy.
    """
    if shutil.which("systemctl") is None:
        return Check("reconcile", "qb-reconcile.timer",
                     "no systemctl on this host, so nothing can say whether the timer runs",
                     "unknown", manual="run qb-reconcile from cron, or install systemd units")
    unit = "qb-reconcile.timer"
    enabled, why_e = unit_state("is-enabled", unit)
    active, why_a = unit_state("is-active", unit)
    fix = ["systemctl", "--user", "enable", "--now", unit]
    # EITHER question failing is an unknown, not a fail. The second cut checked only
    # the first: a bus that went away between the two calls, or a version that
    # answers `is-enabled` from disk and `is-active` from the bus, produced
    # `is-enabled=enabled, is-active=` and reported the timer as broken. Codex found
    # that on the fix for the first one — the same collapse, one call along.
    if why_e or why_a:
        return Check("reconcile", unit, f"systemctl could not answer: {why_e or why_a}",
                     "unknown",
                     manual="check the user bus is up (loginctl enable-linger, or run "
                            "inside a login session) and re-run")
    if enabled == "not-found":
        return Check("reconcile", unit,
                     "no such unit on this host — the units are in harness/loops/systemd/ "
                     "and were never installed", "fail",
                     manual="install harness/loops/systemd/qb-reconcile.{service,timer} into "
                            "~/.config/systemd/user (nix-fleet does this) and "
                            "`systemctl --user daemon-reload`")
    if enabled != "enabled" or active != "active":
        return Check("reconcile", unit, f"is-enabled={enabled}, is-active={active}", "fail",
                     manual="systemctl --user enable --now " + unit, fix=fix)
    return Check("reconcile", unit, f"enabled, active{_last_run()}", "ok")


def _last_run() -> str:
    """`, last run <when>` when systemd will say, and nothing at all when it will not."""
    rc, out, _ = run_cmd(["systemctl", "--user", "show", "qb-reconcile.service",
                          "-p", "ExecMainStartTimestamp", "--value"])
    return f", last run {out}" if rc == 0 and out.strip() else ""


def check_edge(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Can a PERSON write to the board — and can an agent host forge one?

    Two properties, one row, because they are one boundary. `human()` needs an
    edge-asserted `Remote-User` AND the edge's own `X-Edge-Auth` secret, so:

    * the agent vhost must REFUSE a `Remote-User` a caller supplied itself. If it
      ever stopped stripping, any agent on any box could post as Rich. That half
      is checkable from here and it is checked first, because a false pass on it
      is worse than a fail on the other.
    * the browser vhost must ACCEPT the person it authenticated. This was broken
      from v2.39 until 2026-08-22 — `HUMAN_EDGE_SECRET` had never been generated,
      given to the app or injected by nginx — and selfhost #160 fixed it, verified
      live. **Stated in the past tense deliberately.** This paragraph asserted the
      present one for four days after it stopped being true, next to a runbook path
      that had gone stale for the same reason: the issue closing is what moved the
      file, and the same event is what made the sentence wrong.

      It is still `unknown` rather than `ok`, and that is not a leftover. This half
      needs a forward-auth session and an agent host has none, so "nothing on this
      host can tell" remains the honest answer whatever the deployment does — see
      `_edge_human_writes`, and `QUARTERBACK_EDGE_COOKIE` for the one way a box can
      answer it.

    Deliberately NOT done by asking the app whether the secret is configured.
    That would need a new endpoint, and a new endpoint would be absent from the
    deployed image until the very redeploy that fixes the secret — a check that
    cannot answer until after the problem is solved.
    """
    if not host.base_url:
        return Check("edge", "-", "no board configured, so neither vhost can be probed",
                     "unknown")
    spoof = _edge_spoof_refused(host)
    if spoof is not None:
        return spoof
    if not host.human_url:
        return Check("edge", host.base_url,
                     "agent vhost refuses a self-supplied Remote-User; the browser vhost is "
                     "not known to this host, so human writes could not be tested", "unknown",
                     manual="set QUARTERBACK_HUMAN_URL in ~/.config/quarterback/config, then "
                            f"re-run. If human writes are refused, the runbook is {edge_runbook()}")
    return _edge_human_writes(host)


#: The answers that PROVE the agent vhost refused a self-supplied `Remote-User`.
#: A closed set, because the alternative is treating everything-but-200 as proof —
#: which makes a 404 (no such route on an old image) and a 502 (the app is down)
#: read as a security property holding. Found by Codex; it is the same collapse as
#: #324's absent-check-result-as-a-benign-one, in the row where a false pass is
#: worst, since the property it would vouch for is "nobody can forge a person".
_EDGE_REFUSALS = (401, 403)


def _edge_spoof_refused(host: Host) -> Check | None:
    """None when the agent vhost correctly refuses a forged person; a Check when not."""
    status, _body, err = http_get(
        f"{host.base_url}/whoami",
        {"Remote-User": "qb-doctor-probe", "X-Edge-Auth": "qb-doctor-not-the-secret"})
    if err:
        return Check("edge", host.base_url, f"could not reach the agent vhost: {err}", "unknown")
    if status not in (*_EDGE_REFUSALS, 200):
        return Check("edge", host.base_url,
                     f"the agent vhost answered {status} to a forged Remote-User — neither an "
                     "acceptance nor a refusal, so whether it strips the header is untested",
                     "unknown",
                     manual=f"check {host.base_url}/whoami is reachable and serving the board")
    if status == 200:
        return Check("edge", host.base_url,
                     "the agent vhost ACCEPTED a self-supplied Remote-User — anything that "
                     "can reach it can post as a person", "fail",
                     manual="strip Remote-* and X-Edge-Auth on the agent vhost (DEPLOY.md, "
                            "'Security — strip Remote-* on BOTH vhosts'); on a dev board "
                            "this is BROWSER_DEV_HUMAN, which DEPLOY.md's checklist says "
                            "must be unset on a reachable one")
    return None


#: Words that appear in the board's OWN refusal of an unvouched person and in
#: nothing an auth proxy says. `app/auth.py`'s `_NOT_FROM_THE_EDGE` names the header
#: and the setting in full, deliberately — "a message that drifts between the two
#: teaches an operator the wrong fix for whichever one they hit first" — which is
#: what makes it a usable signature here.
_BOARD_REFUSAL_MARKS = ("remote-user", "x-edge-auth", "human_edge_secret", "deploy.md")


def _looks_like_the_boards_refusal(body: str) -> bool:
    """Did the APP refuse this, or did something in front of it?

    A FastAPI refusal is JSON with a `detail`, and the board's own says which
    mechanism it is about. Both are required: an auth portal can serve JSON, and a
    board can serve a `detail` about something else entirely.
    """
    try:
        detail = json.loads(body).get("detail")
    except ValueError:
        return False
    if not isinstance(detail, str):
        return False
    low = detail.lower()
    return any(mark in low for mark in _BOARD_REFUSAL_MARKS)


def _edge_human_writes(host: Host) -> Check:
    """The end-to-end half: does the browser vhost's own person get through?"""
    headers = {}
    cookie = os.environ.get("QUARTERBACK_EDGE_COOKIE")
    if cookie:
        headers["Cookie"] = cookie
    status, body, err = http_get(f"{host.human_url}/whoami", headers)
    if err:
        return Check("edge", host.human_url, f"could not reach the browser vhost: {err}",
                     "unknown", manual=f"runbook: {edge_runbook()}")
    if status in (301, 302, 303, 307, 308):
        return Check(
            "edge", host.human_url,
            f"the browser vhost answered {status} — forward-auth, and this host has no "
            "session, so nothing here can see whether HUMAN_EDGE_SECRET is set", "unknown",
            manual=f"runbook: {edge_runbook()} (or set QUARTERBACK_EDGE_COOKIE to a "
                   "signed-in session and re-run)")
    if status in (401, 403):
        # WHICH hop refused matters, and the status code cannot say. The auth proxy
        # in front of the browser vhost answers 401/403 to a caller with no session,
        # and so does the app to a person the edge did not vouch for — identical
        # codes, opposite diagnoses, and reading the proxy's as the app's would tell
        # somebody the secret is broken when all they are is signed out. Codex found
        # this on the second pass. The board's own refusal names the mechanism in its
        # body, so that is what decides it; anything else is a refusal this tool
        # cannot attribute, which is an unknown.
        if not _looks_like_the_boards_refusal(body):
            return Check("edge", host.human_url,
                         f"the browser vhost answered {status}, and the body is not the "
                         "board's — the auth proxy refused this host before the app saw the "
                         "request, so nothing here can see whether HUMAN_EDGE_SECRET is set",
                         "unknown",
                         manual=f"runbook: {edge_runbook()} (or set QUARTERBACK_EDGE_COOKIE to "
                                "a signed-in session and re-run)")
        return Check("edge", host.human_url,
                     f"human writes are REFUSED ({status}) — the edge authenticated a person "
                     "and the app would not take them, which is HUMAN_EDGE_SECRET unset or "
                     "disagreeing between 1Password and sops", "fail",
                     manual=f"runbook: {edge_runbook()} — needs a generated secret in TWO "
                            "stores and two deploys; that is a person's job, not this tool's")
    if status != 200:
        return Check("edge", host.human_url, f"/whoami answered {status}", "unknown",
                     manual=f"runbook: {edge_runbook()}")
    try:
        kind = json.loads(body).get("kind")
    except ValueError:
        kind = None
    if kind != "human":
        return Check("edge", host.human_url,
                     f"the browser vhost answered as {kind!r}, not a person — Remote-User is "
                     "not reaching the app", "fail",
                     manual=f"runbook: {edge_runbook()}")
    return Check("edge", host.human_url, "human writes accepted", "ok")


def check_tools(host: Host) -> Check:
    """What this host carries that the harness shells out to — and `gh`'s auth.

    The fourth layer from #204's comments: a suite's behaviour depends on host
    facts nothing declares and nothing reports, and isolating one of those cost
    four CI round trips. This does not fix that; it prints it, which is the whole
    of what was missing.
    """
    required = ("git", "gh", "curl", "jq")
    optional = ("docker", "psql", "tmux", "codex", "uv")
    missing = [t for t in required if shutil.which(t) is None]
    have_opt = [t for t in optional if shutil.which(t) is not None]
    detail_tail = (f"; optional present: {', '.join(have_opt) or 'none'}"
                   f"; python {sys.version.split()[0]} at {sys.executable}")
    if missing:
        return Check("tools", "PATH", f"missing: {', '.join(missing)}{detail_tail}", "fail",
                     manual=f"install {', '.join(missing)} on this host",
                     extra={"missing": missing, "optional": have_opt})
    rc, _out, err = run_cmd(["gh", "auth", "status"], timeout=15)
    if rc != 0:
        why = err.splitlines()[0] if err else f"exit {rc}"
        return Check("tools", "PATH", f"gh is not authenticated ({why}){detail_tail}", "fail",
                     manual="gh auth login", extra={"optional": have_opt})
    return Check("tools", "PATH", f"{', '.join(required)} present, gh authenticated{detail_tail}",
                 "ok", extra={"optional": have_opt})


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# can work land? (#406, and the group #407 extends)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

#: `owner/name` out of the shapes a GitHub remote is written in. Anchored at the end
#: so `github.company.com` and `gitlab.com/github/…` are not GitHub — a slug guessed
#: off a host this tool cannot query would make the whole row an `unknown` about the
#: wrong repository.
_GITHUB_REMOTE = re.compile(
    r"^(?:git@github\.com:|ssh://git@github\.com/|https?://(?:[^@/]+@)?github\.com/)"
    r"([^/]+/[^/]+?)(?:\.git)?/?$")

#: The two merge strategies that REWRITE, and the field each is spelled with. Both
#: replace the commits that were pushed with new ones: squash collapses the branch
#: into a fresh commit, rebase replays it onto new parents. Either way the sha that
#: reserved `refs/tags/vX.Y` at push time is not the sha that lands.
_REWRITING = {"allow_squash_merge": "squash", "allow_rebase_merge": "rebase"}


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# does anything here reserve a release tag at push time?
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

#: The call shapes a Python CLI registers a named subcommand with. Enumerated out of
#: the PARSE TREE and never matched in the text, because a docstring, a comment or a
#: dead branch that says `add_parser("reserve")` is not a subcommand — and this repo's
#: own tagger opens with a paragraph explaining that `reserve` is deleted, so a text
#: search would read the file that proves nothing reserves here as proof that something
#: does.
_REGISTRARS = ("add_parser", "add_command", "command")

#: The subset where a first argument this cannot read means the command set was not
#: enumerated. Narrower than `_REGISTRARS` on purpose: `add_parser(x)` is a subcommand
#: named by something, but `.command(x)` is not necessarily a CLI call at all — and
#: `.group(x)` certainly is not, since `m.group(1)` on a regex match is the commonest
#: two-character method call in this repo. Treating those as opacity made
#: `scripts/release_tag.py` unenumerable on the strength of a `re.Match`.
_OPAQUE_IF_UNREADABLE = ("add_parser", "add_command")

#: How a shell script hands its work to another file. A hook that does this has NOT
#: been read by reading it, so either the target is read too or the answer is
#: `unknown`: quarterback's own `pre-push` chains a `pre-push.delegate`, and a
#: reservation performed there is a reservation performed on a push.
_SOURCES = re.compile(r"""^\s*(?:\.|source)\s+(\S+)|(?<![\w-])exec\s+(\S+)""", re.M)

#: Hook runners that keep their own configuration elsewhere. Naming one is enough to
#: make a hook unreadable: what actually runs on a push then lives in `.husky/`,
#: `lefthook.yml` or `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, and this reads none of them.
_HOOK_DISPATCHERS = ("husky", "lefthook", "overcommit")

#: The start of a heredoc, and `(?!<)` is what keeps `<<<"$lines"` — a here-STRING,
#: which this repo's pre-push hook uses twice — from swallowing the rest of the file.
_HEREDOC = re.compile(r"""<<-?\s*(?!<)['"]?(?P<tag>[A-Za-z_]\w*)['"]?""")

#: The bare word, as a command argument. Applied to shell code with its comments,
#: heredoc bodies and quoted spans removed, so `echo "reserve"` and `reason="reserve"`
#: are not reservations. The comment strip is the load-bearing one: this repo's
#: `pre-push` explains the mechanism it no longer has in a block naming
#: `release_tag.py reserve` in full.
_RESERVE_WORD = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.-])reserve(?![\w-])")


def _read(path: Path) -> str | None:
    """A file's text, or None when this host cannot have it."""
    try:
        return path.read_text(errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        return None


def _is_python(path: Path, text: str) -> bool:
    """`.py`, or a shebang naming an interpreter with `python` in it."""
    return path.suffix == ".py" or "python" in text.split("\n", 1)[0]


def _relative(path: Path, repo: Path) -> str:
    """`scripts/release_tag.py` inside the checkout, the full path outside it."""
    try:
        return str(path.relative_to(repo))
    except ValueError:
        return str(path)


def _shell_code(text: str) -> str:
    """A script with its full-line comments and its heredoc bodies removed.

    Whole-line comments only. Guessing where a trailing `#` starts means guessing
    whether it is inside a quoted string, and the quoted spans are taken out
    separately by :func:`_unquoted` anyway.
    """
    out: list[str] = []
    terminator: str | None = None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if terminator is not None:
            if line.strip() == terminator:
                terminator = None
            continue
        if line.lstrip().startswith("#"):
            continue
        out.append(line)
        m = _HEREDOC.search(line)
        if m:
            terminator = m.group("tag")
    return "\n".join(out)


def _unquoted(code: str) -> str:
    """Shell code reduced to the words a shell would RUN: no quoted insides, no comments.

    Two removals, in this order, and the order is what makes the second one safe. With
    the quoted spans already emptied, a `#` cannot be inside a string, so cutting from
    the first one that follows whitespace is exact rather than a guess — and it leaves
    `${var#pattern}` and `$#` alone, since neither `#` follows a space.

    Both removals exist because a false positive on this row is not a wasted question:
    it is a `FAIL` recommending a change to a setting every contributor and every other
    machine in the fleet shares. `echo "reserve"`, `reason="reserve"` and
    `git push  # this used to reserve a tag` are not reservations.

    Only for the word test. Resolving what a script RUNS uses the code with its quotes
    intact, because the file a hook chains to is spelled `"$HOOK_DIR/pre-push.delegate"`.
    """
    bare = re.sub(r"'[^']*'|\"[^\"]*\"", '""', code)
    return re.sub(r"(?m)(?:(?<=\s)|^)#.*$", "", bare)


def _called(node: ast.Call) -> str | None:
    """The bare name of what a call calls — `sub.add_parser(…)` is `add_parser`."""
    fn = node.func
    if isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute):
        return fn.attr
    if isinstance(fn, ast.Name):
        return fn.id
    return None


def _python_subcommands(text: str) -> set[str] | None:
    """Every subcommand a Python file registers, or None when it cannot be told.

    None is the answer to more than a syntax error, and that is the point. A file
    that registers a name this cannot read — `add_parser(COMMAND_RESERVE)`, or a
    subparser handed to some other function to fill in — has not been enumerated,
    and an empty result there would be a check that could not be made rendering as
    one that passed. So the contract is: a set means THESE are the subcommands, and
    None means somebody has to look.
    """
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(text)
    except (SyntaxError, ValueError, RecursionError):
        return None
    holders = {t.id for node in ast.walk(tree)
               if isinstance(node, ast.Assign) and isinstance(node.value, ast.Call)
               and _called(node.value) == "add_subparsers"
               for t in node.targets if isinstance(t, ast.Name)}
    names: set[str] = set()
    opaque = False
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)):
            for dec in node.decorator_list:
                d = dec.func if isinstance(dec, ast.Call) else dec
                bare = d.attr if isinstance(d, ast.Attribute) else (
                    d.id if isinstance(d, ast.Name) else None)
                if bare in ("command", "group"):
                    # `@click.command()` names the function; `@cli.command("reserve")`
                    # names the string, and the Call branch below picks that up too.
                    names.add(node.name)
        if not isinstance(node, ast.Call):
            continue
        called = _called(node)
        if called in _REGISTRARS:
            first = node.args[0] if node.args else None
            if isinstance(first, ast.Constant) and isinstance(first.value, str):
                names.add(first.value)
            elif first is not None and called in _OPAQUE_IF_UNREADABLE:
                opaque = True                       # a subcommand named by something
        elif isinstance(node.func, ast.Call) and _called(node.func) in ("command", "group"):
            # `app.command()(reserve)` — the decorator applied by hand.
            names.update(a.id for a in node.args if isinstance(a, ast.Name))
        elif any(isinstance(a, ast.Name) and a.id in holders for a in node.args):
            # The subparser handed to somebody else to fill in. Whatever it registers
            # is not in this file, so this file does not know the command set.
            opaque = True
    return None if opaque or not names else names


def release_tagger(host: Host) -> tuple[Path | None, str | None]:
    """The tag allocator this repo would use, or why this cannot say.

    Read the way `harness/githooks/pre-push` reads it — `QB_RELEASE_TAG`, then
    `qb.releaseTag`, then `scripts/release_tag.py` — rather than by a rule of this
    file's own. The two have to agree about which repos are in scope, and a second
    predicate here would agree until somebody moved the script.

    Finding one answers WHERE to look and nothing else. Whether it reserves is
    :func:`reservation_sites`' question — the distinction #122 cost, since this repo's
    tagger still exists, with `backfill`, `taken` and `check`, and reserves nothing.

    `git config` exits 1 for a key that is not set and something else for a config it
    could not read. Collapsing the two would turn an unparsable include into "no
    `qb.releaseTag` here", which then falls back to the conventional filename and
    answers confidently about the wrong file.
    """
    from_env = os.environ.get("QB_RELEASE_TAG")
    if from_env:
        return Path(from_env), None
    rc, cfg, err = run_cmd(["git", "-C", str(host.repo), "config", "--get", "qb.releaseTag"])
    if rc not in (0, 1):
        return None, ("this checkout will not say what `qb.releaseTag` is set to "
                      f"({clip_line(err) or f'git config exit {rc}'})")
    if rc == 0 and cfg:
        return (Path(cfg) if cfg.startswith("/") else host.repo / cfg), None
    default = host.repo / "scripts" / "release_tag.py"
    return (default if default.is_file() else None), None


def effective_hooks_dir(host: Host) -> tuple[Path | None, str | None]:
    """Where git will look for this repo's hooks, or why this cannot say.

    `core.hooksPath` when it is set, the common git dir's `hooks/` otherwise — git's
    own precedence, because the point is what RUNS on a push. A hook source sitting in
    `harness/githooks/` reserves nothing.

    A RELATIVE `core.hooksPath` is relative to the directory git runs hooks from, which
    is the top of the working tree — the rule `qb-hooks`' own `effective_delegate`
    states in full. Resolved against this process's cwd instead, it names a directory
    that usually does not exist, and a hook nobody could find would read as a repo with
    no hook at all.
    """
    rc, current, err = run_cmd(
        ["git", "-C", str(host.repo), "config", "--type=path", "--get", "core.hooksPath"])
    if rc not in (0, 1):
        return None, ("this checkout will not say what `core.hooksPath` is set to "
                      f"({clip_line(err) or f'git config exit {rc}'})")
    if rc == 0 and current:
        p = Path(current)
        return (p if p.is_absolute() else host.repo / p), None
    if host.common_git_dir is None:
        return None, "this checkout will not say where git looks for its hooks"
    return host.common_git_dir / "hooks", None


def reservation_sites(host: Host) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """Does anything here take a release tag at PUSH time — and where?

    Returns ``(what reserves, what could not be read)``. Empty both ways means nothing
    here reserves, and that pair is the whole contract: a site this could not read goes
    in the second list rather than being absent from the first. The row turns the
    second list into `unknown`, so every gap in what can be read shows up as one.

    That is the correction #122 forced. The row used to ask whether
    `scripts/release_tag.py` EXISTS, and that file still does, with `backfill`, `taken`
    and `check` and no `reserve` — so it went on reporting `ok` for a reason that had
    stopped being true, which is the failure this whole file is about.

    Two sites, one question. Either is enough for the hazard to be real:

    * the repo's tag allocator exposing a `reserve` subcommand, and
    * the hook git runs on a push carrying a reservation step — including whatever that
      hook chains to, since a reservation in a delegate is a reservation on a push.

    READ, NEVER RUN. Asking `release_tag.py --help` would answer more exactly than any
    parse can, and it would mean a diagnostic executing an unreviewed program out of
    whatever checkout somebody typed it in — the line :func:`load_site_config` already
    draws about the site config. The cost is paid in the second list rather than
    hidden.
    """
    found: list[str] = []
    unreadable: list[str] = []

    tagger, why = release_tagger(host)
    if why:
        unreadable.append(why)
    elif tagger is not None:
        _extend(_tagger_reserves(tagger, host.repo), found, unreadable)

    hooks, why = effective_hooks_dir(host)
    if why:
        unreadable.append(why)
    elif hooks is not None:
        _extend(_hook_reserves(hooks, host.repo), found, unreadable)
    return found, unreadable


def _extend(pair: tuple[list[str], list[str]], found: list[str],
            unreadable: list[str]) -> None:
    found.extend(pair[0])
    unreadable.extend(pair[1])


def _tagger_reserves(tagger: Path, repo: Path) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """One tag allocator, read."""
    rel = _relative(tagger, repo)
    text = _read(tagger)
    if text is None:
        return [], [f"the tag allocator at {rel} could not be read"]
    if _is_python(tagger, text):
        names = _python_subcommands(text)
        if names is None:
            return [], [f"the tag allocator at {rel} does not declare its subcommands "
                        "in a shape this can enumerate"]
        return ([f"{rel} exposes a reserve subcommand"] if "reserve" in names else []), []
    # Not Python, so it is a wrapper around something, and its command set is wherever
    # that something is. Finding the word is evidence; not finding it is not evidence
    # of the opposite, so the absent case is `unknown` and not a pass.
    if _RESERVE_WORD.search(_unquoted(_shell_code(text))):
        return [f"{rel} has a reserve step"], []
    return [], [f"the tag allocator at {rel} is not Python, so what it exposes cannot be "
                "enumerated from it"]


def _hook_reserves(hooks: Path, repo: Path) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """The pre-push hook git would run here, and everything it hands work to.

    `pre-push.delegate` is followed by name because that is the convention `qb-hooks`
    installs — it symlinks one in whenever the machine already had a `pre-push` to keep
    running, and the managed hook pipes the push into it. A reservation performed there
    happens on exactly the pushes this row is about.
    """
    entry = hooks / "pre-push"
    if not entry.is_file():
        return [], []                     # git runs no pre-push here, so none reserves
    queue = [entry]
    delegate = hooks / "pre-push.delegate"
    if delegate.is_file():
        queue.append(delegate)
    seen: set[Path] = set()
    found: list[str] = []
    unreadable: list[str] = []
    while queue:
        path = queue.pop()
        resolved = path.resolve()
        if resolved in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(resolved)
        where = ("the pre-push hook installed here" if path == entry
                 else f"{path.name}, which the pre-push hook here runs,")
        text = _read(path)
        if text is None:
            unreadable.append(f"{where} could not be read")
            continue
        code = _shell_code(text)
        for runner in _HOOK_DISPATCHERS:
            if re.search(rf"(?<![\w-]){runner}(?![\w-])", _unquoted(code)):
                unreadable.append(f"{where} hands its work to {runner}, whose own "
                                  "configuration this does not read")
        for target in _runs(code):
            if "$" in target or "`" in target:
                # Spelled with a variable or a substitution: this script hands its work
                # somewhere that was not read, and what it does there is not known.
                unreadable.append(f"{where} runs `{target}`, which this cannot resolve "
                                  "to a file to read")
                continue
            nxt = _resolve_run(target, hooks, repo)
            if nxt is not None:
                queue.append(nxt)
        if _RESERVE_WORD.search(_unquoted(code)):
            found.append(f"{where.rstrip(',')} has a reservation step")
    return found, unreadable


def _runs(code: str) -> list[str]:
    """Every file a script sources or execs, as written."""
    return [a or b for a, b in _SOURCES.findall(code) if (a or b)]


def _resolve_run(target: str, hooks: Path, repo: Path) -> Path | None:
    """The file a literal `source`/`exec` target names, or None when there is none.

    A concrete path that is not there is not an unknown: nothing runs, so there is
    nothing to read and nothing to report. Tried against the hooks dir and against the
    top of the working tree, which are the two directories a hook's own relative paths
    are written from.
    """
    word = target.strip("\"'")
    if word.startswith("-") or ">" in word or "&" in word:
        return None                        # `exec 3>&1` and friends name no file
    if Path(word).is_absolute():
        return Path(word) if Path(word).is_file() else None
    for base in (hooks, repo):
        if (base / word).is_file():
            return base / word
    return None


def github_slug(host: Host) -> str | None:
    """`owner/name` for this checkout's remote, or None when it is not GitHub."""
    rc, url, _ = run_cmd(["git", "-C", str(host.repo), "remote", "get-url", host.remote])
    if rc != 0 or not url:
        return None
    m = _GITHUB_REMOTE.match(url.strip())
    return m.group(1) if m else None


def check_merges(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Can a merge into this repo rewrite the commit a release tag was reserved against?

    #406. `v3.8` was squash-merged while every other landing that night used a merge
    commit. A tag reserved at PUSH time names the commit that was pushed, and a squash
    discards it — so `refs/tags/v3.8` addressed a commit that is not in main's history
    while the v3.8 entry landed on main perfectly, and the guard called `every release
    on main has a tag` stayed green because a tag of that name resolved. Rebase-merge
    has the identical defect for the identical reason; both are checked, because
    disabling only the one that happened to bite would leave the other waiting.

    THE QUESTION IS WHETHER ANYTHING RESERVES, not whether a file exists. The row as it
    first landed asked whether `scripts/release_tag.py` was there, and #122 removed
    push-time reservation from this repo within twelve hours while leaving that file in
    place with `backfill`, `taken` and `check`. So it went on reporting `ok` for a
    reason that had stopped being true, and its `fail` text would have told a reader to
    switch off squash merges to protect a reservation nothing takes. The predicate is
    :func:`reservation_sites` now: a `reserve` subcommand on the tagger, or a
    reservation step in the hook git runs on a push.

    Which keeps the row USEFUL RATHER THAN DELETED. The harness installs into repos
    that are not quarterback, and one still carrying a pre-#122 `release_tag.py` has
    the original defect exactly as it was written. The row finds that repo and stays
    quiet here.

    NOT ASKED WHERE IT DOES NOT APPLY. A repo that reserves nothing has no reservation
    for a merge to orphan, and a `FAIL` a reader cannot act on is how a row gets
    ignored. Two things it deliberately does not grow into. Whether the release number
    is applied in the right PLACE is #122's question and belongs to #122's rows — one
    row with two premises is how this one drifted. And whether a squash should discard
    a branch's reviewed history at all is a policy about review rather than about tags;
    it is a real question and it wants its own row and its own argument.

    DETECT, DO NOT SET, and that is a decision rather than the easier half. Every
    `fix` this tool runs writes to THIS host: a hooks directory, a systemd unit,
    a venv. This one would write to a setting every contributor and every other
    machine in the fleet shares, from whichever checkout somebody happened to type
    `--fix` in, and it needs repository-admin rights a read-only token does not have.
    It is `manual` for the reason the edge secret is: the remedy belongs to a person,
    so this prints the exact one-line command and runs nothing.
    """
    reserves, unreadable = reservation_sites(host)
    if not reserves and unreadable:
        return Check("merges", str(host.repo),
                     f"{unreadable[0]}, so whether anything here takes a release tag at push "
                     "time — the reservation a squash or rebase merge would orphan — could "
                     "not be established", "unknown",
                     manual="read the repo's release tagger and its installed pre-push hook "
                            "by hand: if either reserves a tag at push time, the forge must "
                            "allow merge commits only",
                     extra={"unreadable": unreadable})
    if not reserves:
        # And it stops there. "Nothing reserves a tag at push time" is what was
        # established; where the number IS applied is a different question with a
        # different owner (#122's rows), and a verdict that asserted it would be this
        # row growing the second premise it was just corrected for.
        return Check("merges", str(host.repo),
                     "nothing here reserves a release tag at push time, so a rewriting merge "
                     "has no reservation to orphan", "ok", extra={"reserves": []})
    because = " and ".join(reserves)
    slug = github_slug(host)
    if slug is None:
        return Check("merges", host.remote,
                     f"{because}, and {host.remote} is not a github.com remote, so the merge "
                     "strategies this repo allows cannot be read from here", "unknown",
                     manual="check the forge's merge settings by hand: a squash or rebase "
                            "merge orphans the tag reserved at push time")
    if shutil.which("gh") is None:
        return Check("merges", slug, f"{because}, and there is no gh on this host, so nothing "
                     "can ask GitHub which merge strategies it allows", "unknown",
                     manual="install gh and `gh auth login` (see the tools row)")
    # `push` comes back in the same call because it is what makes the absent case
    # legible: GitHub sends the merge-strategy fields ONLY to a token with push
    # access, so a read-only token gets three nulls and no explanation. Asking for
    # both means the row can say which of the two silences it is looking at.
    rc, out, err = run_cmd(
        ["gh", "api", f"repos/{slug}", "--jq",
         "{allow_merge_commit, allow_squash_merge, allow_rebase_merge, "
         "push: .permissions.push}"], timeout=15)
    if rc != 0:
        return Check("merges", slug, f"gh could not read the repo settings: "
                     f"{clip_line(err) or f'exit {rc}'}", "unknown",
                     manual=f"gh auth status, then `gh api repos/{slug}`")
    try:
        settings = json.loads(out)
    except ValueError:
        settings = None
    # `null`, `[]` and `"rate limited"` are all valid JSON, so parsing is not the test —
    # an object with the fields in it is. Codex found this: without the isinstance the
    # row still came out `unknown`, but through `run_checks` catching an AttributeError,
    # which reports "the check itself raised" instead of the remedy this branch prints.
    if not isinstance(settings, dict):
        return Check("merges", slug, "gh did not answer an object describing the repo "
                     "settings", "unknown", manual=f"gh api repos/{slug}")
    return _merge_verdict(slug, settings, because)


def _merge_verdict(slug: str, settings: dict, because: str) -> Check:
    """The four answers, given what GitHub said about one repo that DOES reserve.

    `because` names the reservation site this row already found, and every verdict
    carries it, because the reader deciding whether to switch off a merge strategy is
    entitled to see what the setting is protecting.

    A field GitHub did not send is `unknown` and never `ok`. A read-only token, a
    GHES old enough to omit one, or a `--jq` that stopped matching would otherwise
    read as the setting being off — the #324 collapse, in the row whose whole job is
    to notice that a switch is on.
    """
    absent = [k for k in ("allow_merge_commit", *_REWRITING) if settings.get(k) is None]
    if absent and not settings.get("push"):
        # The common one, and it is not a defect in the repo. GitHub returns these
        # fields only to a token with push access; without it the answer is three
        # nulls, which is why the remedy is somebody else's token rather than another
        # run of the same command.
        return Check("merges", slug,
                     f"this token has no push access to {slug}, and GitHub states the merge "
                     "strategies only to one that does — so nothing here can say whether a "
                     "squash merge is allowed", "unknown",
                     manual=f"ask somebody with push access on {slug} to run `gh api "
                            f"repos/{slug} --jq '.allow_squash_merge, .allow_rebase_merge'`")
    if absent:
        return Check("merges", slug,
                     f"GitHub did not say whether this repo allows {', '.join(absent)}, so "
                     "the merge strategy is untested", "unknown",
                     manual=f"gh api repos/{slug} --jq '{{{', '.join(absent)}}}'")
    fix_cmd = (f"gh api -X PATCH repos/{slug} -F allow_squash_merge=false "
               "-F allow_rebase_merge=false -F allow_merge_commit=true")
    allowed = sorted(word for key, word in _REWRITING.items() if settings[key])
    if allowed:
        return Check("merges", slug,
                     f"this repo allows {' and '.join(allowed)} merges, and {because} — a "
                     "rewriting merge discards the commit the tag was reserved against "
                     "(#406, v3.8)", "fail", manual=fix_cmd,
                     extra={"allows": allowed, "settings": settings, "reserves": because})
    # The property is "every landing preserves the commit that was pushed", which needs
    # both halves: the rewriting strategies off, AND one left that does not rewrite.
    # GitHub will not currently let all three be false, so this branch is a guard rather
    # than a case anybody has hit — and it is here because the row states the property,
    # not the two settings it happens to have looked at.
    if not settings["allow_merge_commit"]:
        return Check("merges", slug,
                     "this repo allows no merge strategy that preserves the pushed commits, "
                     "so every landing rewrites the release commit its tag names", "fail",
                     manual=fix_cmd, extra={"settings": settings})
    return Check("merges", slug, f"merge commits only — squash and rebase are both off, so a "
                 f"landing cannot orphan the tag reserved at push time ({because})", "ok",
                 extra={"settings": settings, "reserves": because})


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# can work land? the rest of the group (#407, #122)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

#: How long a queue may hold entries with none ready before that is a finding. Not a
#: guess: PR #398 was landed twice and timed, at 5m37s and 12m59s from merge to green,
#: and #405's stall ran for over three hours. Fifteen minutes sits inside the noise of
#: one slow landing; thirty is more than double the slowest measured one and still turns
#: a three-hour stall into a half-hour question.
QUEUE_STALL_MINUTES = 30

#: How long the integration branch may stand still WHILE something is ready to land on
#: it. The condition is what makes the number safe — a quiet `main` at four in the
#: morning is not a fault, and a `main` that has not moved for two hours with green
#: pull requests waiting is the single most legible statement of #405's night.
MAIN_STALL_MINUTES = 120

#: How many open pull requests the GitHub-backed rows read. A cap, because these run on a
#: command a person types when something feels wrong and `gh` pages until it has them all.
#: Reaching it is NOT reported and moved on from: a row that saw the first N and found
#: nothing has not established that there is nothing, so it says `unknown`. Codex found
#: that on the first cut — the cap was disclosed in the detail and the verdict was still
#: `ok`, which is the disclosure making the false green sound careful.
#:
#: A finding among the ones that WERE read is still a finding, so the fail branches come
#: first: truncation cannot make a pull request that edits CHANGELOG.md stop editing it.
PR_SAMPLE = 200

#: The fields both GitHub-backed rows ask for. One list rather than two overlapping ones,
#: so the answer can be cached and the two rows cannot come to describe the same pull
#: requests differently.
_PR_FIELDS = "number,isDraft,files,mergeStateStatus"

#: What `mergeStateStatus` says when GitHub has computed that a merge would succeed right
#: now. Used instead of a rollup of check conclusions because it is GitHub's own answer to
#: the question the row is asking, and a hand-rolled "are all the checks green" agrees
#: with it until somebody adds a required review or a merge-queue rule.
_READY_TO_LAND = "CLEAN"

#: And what it says when it has not computed one yet. GitHub resolves this lazily; a PR
#: nobody has asked about since its last push answers UNKNOWN, which is not evidence that
#: it is unready.
_STATE_UNKNOWN = ("UNKNOWN", "", None)

#: The file the release job writes and no branch may edit (#122, decision (a)). The
#: README's release list is guarded too, and is deliberately NOT looked for here: the
#: guard exempts the rest of README.md so that documentation is not taxed, and a list of
#: changed paths cannot tell an edit to the release list from an edit to anything else in
#: the file. Naming README.md here would fail a branch for improving its own docs.
GENERATED_BY_THE_RELEASE_JOB = ("CHANGELOG.md",)

#: A code fence in a brief running one of these is a document telling the next agent to do
#: the thing #122 removed. Kept in step with `test_no_brief_tells_an_agent_to_stamp_a_release`
#: by saying so in both places: the test pins the briefs this repo ships, and this row asks
#: the same question of the briefs INSTALLED on this host, which is a different set the
#: moment the harness on PATH falls behind.
_STAMPING_COMMANDS = (
    r"release_stamp\.py",                          # the tool itself, gone since #122
    r"changelog_fragments\.py\"?\s+assemble",      # the branch-side entry builder
    r"release_tag\.py\"?\s+reserve",               # the push-time number reservation
    r"release\.py\"?\s+run",                       # the release job — `main` only
)

#: A fenced block in a brief. The leading `[ \t]*` is not cosmetic and the same pattern in
#: `test_commands_wired.py` says why: every fence in `fix-and-review.md` sits inside a
#: numbered step and is indented, so an anchored ``` matches nothing at all — and "no
#: forbidden command in any code block" over zero code blocks passes on every file.
_FENCE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*```[^\n]*\n(.*?)^[ \t]*```", re.DOTALL | re.MULTILINE)


def commands_dir(harness: Path) -> Path | None:
    """Where the briefs an agent actually reads live, in the two layouts that exist.

    The same two places :func:`githooks_dir` looks, for the same reason: a checkout keeps
    `bin/` and `commands/` as siblings, and the package puts the briefs under
    `share/quarterback-harness/`. Hard-coding either would make this row quietly stop
    reading anything on half the fleet.
    """
    for cand in (harness / "commands", harness / "share" / "quarterback-harness" / "commands"):
        if cand.is_dir():
            return cand
    return None


def installed_harness(host: Host) -> Path:
    """The harness tree whose briefs this host would actually open.

    The one on PATH when there is one, and this script's own otherwise — the rule the
    rest of this file follows. It matters here more than anywhere: the `harness` row
    exists because the harness on PATH goes stale, and a stale harness ships stale
    briefs. Reading the checkout's briefs while an agent on this box reads the store's
    would report the repository's intentions rather than the machine's behaviour.
    """
    return host.harness_bin.parent if host.harness_bin else host.source_harness


def fragments_in_use(host: Host) -> bool:
    """Does this repo do releases out of `changelog.d/` fragments?

    The scope test for both #122 rows, and it is a directory rather than a config flag
    because that is what `changelog.d/README.md` says the contract is. A repo whose
    branches legitimately write their own release entry is not failing anything.
    """
    return (host.repo / "changelog.d").is_dir()


def _board_json(host: Host, path: str) -> tuple[object | None, str]:
    """One authenticated GET against the board, decoded — or a sentence saying why not.

    Returns ``(payload, "")`` or ``(None, why)``, with the payload exactly as the board
    shaped it. Nothing here judges that shape, because the board does not answer one:
    `/blockers` returns an object and `/board` returns a bare array, and a single helper
    that insisted on either turned every caller of the other into "could not be asked"
    without ever reaching the question it was asked to settle (#531).
    """
    if not host.base_url:
        return None, "no board is configured (QUARTERBACK_BASE_URL unset), so nothing here " \
                     "can read the queue"
    if not host.token:
        return None, "this machine resolved no board token, so the queue cannot be read " \
                     "(the token row says which of the three sources is empty)"
    status, body, err = http_get(f"{host.base_url}{path}",
                                 {"Authorization": f"Bearer {host.token}"})
    if err:
        return None, f"could not reach the board: {err}"
    if status != 200:
        return None, f"the board answered {status} to {path}"
    try:
        return json.loads(body), ""
    except ValueError:
        return None, f"the board did not answer JSON to {path}"


def board_get(host: Host, path: str) -> tuple[dict | None, str]:
    """`_board_json`, for the board endpoints that answer a JSON object.

    Every caller turns the `why` into an `unknown`, so there is one place where "the
    board could not be asked" is phrased and no way for a row to reach a verdict without
    having been answered.
    """
    # `if why`, not `if payload is None`: a body of `null` decodes to None with nothing
    # wrong with the request, and testing the payload would hand back an EMPTY reason
    # for it — a blank cell in the table where a sentence belongs. The failure is
    # carried by `why` alone, so `null` falls through to the shape check below and gets
    # the same sentence as any other answer of the wrong shape.
    payload, why = _board_json(host, path)
    if why:
        return None, why
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return None, f"the board did not answer an object to {path}"
    return payload, ""


def board_get_list(host: Host, path: str) -> tuple[list | None, str]:
    """`_board_json`, for the board endpoints that answer a JSON array.

    `GET /board` is one — `read_board` is annotated `-> list[dict]` and returns the posts
    bare. The `{"posts": [...], "cursor": N}` object is assembled by the MCP `board_read`
    wrapper on the way out, so it is the shape an agent sees and NOT the shape the API
    has, and a row written against it cannot read a single post (#531).
    """
    # `if why`, not `if payload is None`: a body of `null` decodes to None with nothing
    # wrong with the request, and testing the payload would hand back an EMPTY reason
    # for it — a blank cell in the table where a sentence belongs. The failure is
    # carried by `why` alone, so `null` falls through to the shape check below and gets
    # the same sentence as any other answer of the wrong shape.
    payload, why = _board_json(host, path)
    if why:
        return None, why
    if not isinstance(payload, list):
        return None, f"the board did not answer a list to {path}"
    return payload, ""


def _age_minutes(stamp: str | None) -> float | None:
    """Minutes since an ISO-8601 instant, or None when it cannot be read as one."""
    if not isinstance(stamp, str) or not stamp:
        return None
    try:
        when = datetime.fromisoformat(stamp.replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if when.tzinfo is None:
        when = when.replace(tzinfo=UTC)
    # Negative is returned rather than clamped, so a caller can tell "just now" from "this
    # instant is in the future", which is clock skew or bad data and not evidence of health.
    return (datetime.now(UTC) - when).total_seconds() / 60


def _sane_counts(counts: object) -> bool:
    """Is this a pair of queue counts that could describe a real queue?

    Presence and type are not enough, and Codex found both holes. `bool` is an `int` in
    Python, so `{"queued": True}` typechecks; and `ready > queued`, or a negative either
    way, is a board answering something no queue can be in. The row's `ok` branches read
    those numbers, so an answer that cannot be true has to be an `unknown` — the same
    rule as a field GitHub did not send.
    """
    if not isinstance(counts, dict):
        return False
    queued, ready = counts.get("queued"), counts.get("ready")
    for n in (queued, ready):
        if not isinstance(n, int) or isinstance(n, bool) or n < 0:
            return False
    return ready <= queued


def _spell_age(minutes: float) -> str:
    """`14m`, `3h 12m` — short enough for a report row, exact enough to quote."""
    total = int(minutes)
    return f"{total}m" if total < 60 else f"{total // 60}h {total % 60:02d}m"


def check_queue(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Is the line moving, or is everything in it waiting on nothing?

    #405, and the cheapest row in this group. On the night of 2026-08-22/23 the merge
    queue held seven green pull requests with ZERO ready and `main` did not move for over
    three hours, while this tool reported nine `ok` and one `unknown`. Every row it had
    was correct and not one of them was about the line.

    `queued > 0 && ready == 0` is the whole predicate, plus a clock. Both halves are
    needed: nothing ready is the normal state of a queue whose head is mid-preland, and
    an empty queue on a repo with no open pull requests is not a fault. What is a fault is
    that pair holding for longer than a landing takes.

    ADVISORY, and this row does not soften that. #405 argued the queue must stay advisory
    and the argument held under measurement: the system was never wrong about who should
    land next, only about who was still there. So nothing here evicts an entry, re-orders
    the line or merges anything — it says the line has stopped, and names the head so
    there is somebody to ask.
    """
    slug = github_slug(host)
    if slug is None:
        return Check("queue", host.remote,
                     f"{host.remote} is not a github.com remote, so this checkout does not "
                     "name a queue the board would recognise", "unknown",
                     manual="read the queue by hand: GET /merge-queue?repo=…&base=…")
    base = _default_branch(host)
    if base is None:
        return Check("queue", slug, f"could not resolve {host.remote}'s default branch, and "
                     "the queue is keyed by the branch being landed onto", "unknown",
                     manual=f"git -C {host.repo} remote set-head {host.remote} --auto")
    payload, why = board_get(host, f"/merge-queue?repo={quote(slug)}&base={quote(base)}")
    if payload is None:
        return Check("queue", host.base_url or "-", why, "unknown",
                     manual="see the board and token rows")
    counts = payload.get("counts")
    if not _sane_counts(counts):
        return Check("queue", slug, "the board answered without a usable pair of queue "
                     "counts, so whether anything can land could not be read", "unknown",
                     manual=f"{host.base_url}/merge-queue?repo={slug}&base={base}")
    queued, ready = counts["queued"], counts["ready"]
    subject = f"{slug}@{base}"
    if queued == 0:
        return Check("queue", subject, f"nothing is queued to land on {base}", "ok",
                     extra={"queued": 0, "ready": 0})
    if ready > 0:
        return Check("queue", subject, f"{queued} queued on {base}, {ready} ready to land",
                     "ok", extra={"queued": queued, "ready": ready})
    entries = payload.get("entries") or []
    ages = [a for a in (_age_minutes((e or {}).get("entered")) for e in entries)
            if a is not None and a >= 0]
    if not ages:
        return Check("queue", subject,
                     f"{queued} queued on {base} with none ready, and the board stated no "
                     "usable arrival time for any of them — so how long that has been true "
                     "could not be read", "unknown",
                     manual=f"{host.base_url}/merge-queue?repo={slug}&base={base}")
    waited = max(ages)
    head = (payload.get("head") or {})
    who = head.get("holder") or "nobody named"
    pr = head.get("pr")
    if waited >= QUEUE_STALL_MINUTES:
        return Check("queue", subject,
                     f"{queued} queued on {base} and NONE ready — the oldest has waited "
                     f"{_spell_age(waited)}, so nothing in the line can move", "fail",
                     manual=f"ask {who}, who holds the head (PR #{pr}): a lapsed entry is "
                            "returned to the back of the line and may have been conflicted "
                            f"by whatever landed ahead of it (#405). {host.base_url}"
                            f"/merge-queue?repo={slug}&base={base}",
                     extra={"queued": queued, "ready": 0, "waited_minutes": int(waited),
                            "head_pr": pr, "head_holder": who})
    return Check("queue", subject,
                 f"{queued} queued on {base}, none ready yet — the oldest has waited "
                 f"{_spell_age(waited)}, which is inside a landing", "ok",
                 extra={"queued": queued, "ready": 0, "waited_minutes": int(waited)})


def open_pull_requests(host: Host) -> tuple[list[dict] | None, str]:
    """The open pull requests on this repo, or a sentence saying why not.

    One place, because two rows need them and each would otherwise invent its own way of
    not being able to reach GitHub — and its own field list, which is how two rows come
    to describe the same pull requests differently. Cached on the Host so a plain run
    makes one call and not two.

    A list of exactly `PR_SAMPLE` is a TRUNCATED list, and callers are told so through
    :func:`truncated` rather than left to compare lengths. Its `ok` branches have to
    become `unknown`; its finding branches do not, since a pull request that was read and
    is wrong does not stop being wrong because others were not read.
    """
    slug = github_slug(host)
    if slug is None:
        return None, (f"{host.remote} is not a github.com remote, so the open pull requests "
                      "cannot be read from here")
    if shutil.which("gh") is None:
        return None, "no gh on this host, so nothing can ask GitHub about the open pull requests"
    if "prs" in host.gh_cache:
        return host.gh_cache["prs"]
    rc, out, err = run_cmd(["gh", "pr", "list", "--repo", slug, "--state", "open",
                            "--limit", str(PR_SAMPLE), "--json", _PR_FIELDS], timeout=60)
    if rc != 0:
        answer = (None, f"gh could not list the pull requests: "
                        f"{clip_line(err) or f'exit {rc}'}")
    else:
        try:
            prs = json.loads(out)
        except ValueError:
            prs = None
        answer = ((prs, "") if isinstance(prs, list)
                  else (None, "gh did not answer a list of pull requests"))
    host.gh_cache["prs"] = answer
    return answer


def truncated(prs: list[dict]) -> bool:
    """Did the read stop at the cap? Then this is a sample and not the population."""
    return len(prs) >= PR_SAMPLE


def check_landed(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Has the integration branch moved, given what is ready to land on it?

    "Six green pull requests, main unchanged for four hours" is the one-line statement of
    the night #407 was filed, and no row anywhere made it. The two halves have to be asked
    together: a quiet `main` is not a fault on its own, and a green pull request is not a
    fault on its own. Together they are the definition of work that cannot land.

    Readiness is `mergeStateStatus == CLEAN`, which is GitHub's own answer to "would a
    merge succeed right now". Rolling up check conclusions here would agree with it until
    somebody added a required review or a merge-queue rule, and then disagree silently.
    GitHub computes it lazily, so `UNKNOWN` is a pull request nobody has asked about since
    its last push — counted as unknown and never as unready.

    WHAT "MOVED" MEANS HERE, EXACTLY, because the honest name for it is not the obvious
    one. GitHub's REST API states no ref-update time for a branch, so what is read is the
    COMMITTER DATE OF THE TIP COMMIT, and that is what the verdict says. For a repo that
    lands with merge commits the two are the same instant, and this repo's `merges` row is
    about keeping it that way. Where they part is a fast-forward of a commit made earlier
    — that reads as an older move than it was, so this row can ask its question early on a
    repo that fast-forwards. It cannot read late, which is the direction that would matter.
    """
    slug = github_slug(host)
    base = _default_branch(host)
    if slug is None or base is None:
        return Check("landed", host.remote,
                     "this checkout does not resolve to a GitHub repo and default branch, so "
                     "whether the branch that work lands on is moving cannot be asked",
                     "unknown", manual=f"git -C {host.repo} remote -v")
    prs, why = open_pull_requests(host)
    if prs is None:
        return Check("landed", slug, why, "unknown", manual="see the tools row for gh")
    rc, when, err = run_cmd(["gh", "api", f"repos/{slug}/commits/{quote(base)}",
                             "--jq", ".commit.committer.date"], timeout=20)
    age = _age_minutes(when) if rc == 0 else None
    if age is None or age < 0:
        return Check("landed", slug, f"could not read when the tip of {base} was committed: "
                     f"{clip_line(err) or when or f'exit {rc}'}", "unknown",
                     manual=f"gh api repos/{slug}/commits/{base}")
    ready = [p["number"] for p in prs
             if not p.get("isDraft") and p.get("mergeStateStatus") == _READY_TO_LAND]
    unsaid = [p["number"] for p in prs
              if not p.get("isDraft") and p.get("mergeStateStatus") in _STATE_UNKNOWN]
    moved = f"the tip of {base} was committed {_spell_age(age)} ago"
    # The finding first, and deliberately before the truncation guard: a pull request that
    # was read and is ready does not stop being ready because others were not read.
    if ready and age >= MAIN_STALL_MINUTES:
        return Check("landed", slug,
                     f"{_spell_prs(ready)} ready to land and {moved} — work that is finished "
                     "is not reaching the branch it has to reach", "fail",
                     manual="read the queue row, then `gh pr view <n>` on the head of the "
                            "line: the queue is advisory and a person landing one of these "
                            "by hand is a legitimate move (#405)",
                     extra={"open": len(prs), "ready": ready, "tip_age_minutes": int(age)})
    if truncated(prs):
        return Check("landed", slug,
                     f"{moved}, and this read the first {PR_SAMPLE} open pull requests and "
                     "stopped — so whether anything beyond them is ready to land could not "
                     "be established", "unknown",
                     manual=f"gh pr list --repo {slug} --state open --limit 1000 --json "
                            "number,mergeStateStatus")
    if not ready and unsaid:
        return Check("landed", slug,
                     f"{moved}, and GitHub has not yet computed a merge state for "
                     f"{_spell_prs(unsaid)} — so whether anything is ready to land could "
                     "not be read", "unknown",
                     manual=f"gh pr list --repo {slug} --json number,mergeStateStatus "
                            "(GitHub computes it lazily; asking again usually settles it)")
    if not ready:
        return Check("landed", slug, f"{moved}; nothing among {len(prs)} open pull "
                     f"request(s) is ready to land, so a still {base} is not a stall", "ok",
                     extra={"open": len(prs), "ready": 0, "tip_age_minutes": int(age)})
    return Check("landed", slug, f"{_spell_prs(ready)} ready to land, and {moved}", "ok",
                 extra={"open": len(prs), "ready": ready, "tip_age_minutes": int(age)})


def _spell_prs(numbers: list[int]) -> str:
    """`3 pull requests (#401, #403, #404)`, capped so a row stays a row."""
    shown = ", ".join(f"#{n}" for n in numbers[:6])
    more = "" if len(numbers) <= 6 else f", +{len(numbers) - 6} more"
    word = "pull request" if len(numbers) == 1 else "pull requests"
    return f"{len(numbers)} {word} ({shown}{more})"


#: The API ceiling for `/board` and `/blockers` alike. Named because the `escalations`
#: row has to REASON about it: a full page is not the same fact on the two endpoints —
#: `/board` pages newest-first, so a full page still contains the whole window, while
#: `/blockers` pages oldest-first, where a full page can exclude the window entirely.
_BOARD_PAGE = 1000

#: The window both halves of the `escalations` row are counted over. 24h is not a
#: tuning choice — it is the largest `window_min` the board's own read accepts, and
#: the two counts have to come from the same span or the comparison says nothing.
ESCALATION_WINDOW_MINUTES = 1440

#: The post type `needs_human.announce()` writes. Named here rather than spelled at
#: the call site because this row's entire claim is that these two writes come from
#: one function, and a typo would make it report a severed producer forever.
ESCALATION_POST = "stuck"


def check_escalations(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Is the escalation path writing rows, or only ringing the bell?

    #523, and the row exists because this fleet has now built the same empty queue
    three times. The `stuck` post type measured at ZERO over thirty days. Six
    `needs-human/*` labels shipped with no producer, no consumer and no test. #328
    landed a `blockers` table and no producer. None of the three was the wrong shape
    and nothing ever wrote to them — which is a failure with no symptom, because an
    empty queue and a working queue with nothing in it look identical from outside.

    So the question is asked as a PAIR, the way `landed` asks its own. A blockers
    table with nothing in it is not a fault: it is the correct state of a day nobody
    got stuck. `stuck` posts with nothing in the table is the fault, and it is only
    visible because ONE function makes both writes — `announce()` posts and then
    stores, so a post without a row in the same window is that function's second
    half failing, or never having been deployed.

    STRICTLY ANY-VERSUS-NONE, and not a ratio, because rows are legitimately fewer
    than posts. An escalation whose refs name no subject is announced and
    deliberately not stored (#523), so `posts > rows` is the designed behaviour and
    a proportional test here would fail on correct data. Zero rows against any post
    is the only divergence that cannot be explained by the design.

    A board with no `/blockers` at all reads `unknown`, not `fail`: an image
    predating #328 has not severed anything, it has not been redeployed, and those
    are different sentences to be handed.
    """
    window = ESCALATION_WINDOW_MINUTES
    # A LIST, because that is what `GET /board` answers. Reading it as an object is the
    # whole of #531: this row shipped unable to see a single post, and said so in a
    # sentence — "the board did not answer an object" — that pointed at the `board` and
    # `token` rows, both of which were `ok` and neither of which was the fault.
    posts, why = board_get_list(
        host,
        f"/board?type={ESCALATION_POST}&window_min={window}&limit={_BOARD_PAGE}")
    if posts is None:
        return Check("escalations", host.base_url or "-", why, "unknown",
                     manual="see the board and token rows")
    # Dated HERE, because `window_min` is a request and not a promise: `/board` floors a
    # quiet window at the ten most recent posts of the slice whatever their age
    # (_ORIENT_FLOOR, app/api/posts.py), and `type=` is deliberately not one of the
    # lookups that skip the floor. So asking for a day and being handed last week's ten
    # makes `raised` non-empty on a day nobody escalated — and this row would read that
    # as a severed producer and say so, on the evidence of posts that predate the
    # question. Observed: the live board answered exactly ten, five of them older than
    # the cutoff. The blocker half below has always dated its own rows; both halves now
    # describe the same span, which is the only way their comparison means anything.
    #
    # `isinstance` before `.get`, on both halves: a list whose ELEMENTS are not objects
    # is a board answering nonsense, and a diagnostic that raises AttributeError while
    # diagnosing is worth less than one that reports what it found.
    raised = [p for p in posts
              if isinstance(p, dict)
              and (age := _age_minutes(p.get("ts"))) is not None
              and 0 <= age <= window]
    rows, why = board_get(host, f"/blockers?open=false&limit={_BOARD_PAGE}")
    if rows is None:
        # Told apart from any other board failure, because the answer is a deploy and
        # not a bug: `board_get` turns every non-200 into this one sentence shape.
        missing = "404" in why
        return Check("escalations", host.base_url or "-",
                     ("this board serves no /blockers, so it is running an image from "
                      "before #328 and the row half of an escalation has nowhere to land "
                      f"— {len(raised)} {ESCALATION_POST} post(s) in the last "
                      f"{_spell_age(window)} went to the board as posts only")
                     if missing else why, "unknown",
                     manual="redeploy the board (#328/#519), then re-run this row")
    stored = rows.get("blockers")
    if not isinstance(stored, list):
        return Check("escalations", host.base_url or "-",
                     "the board answered no list of blockers, so what the escalation path "
                     "recorded could not be read", "unknown",
                     manual=f"{host.base_url}/blockers?open=false")
    span = _spell_age(window)
    if len(stored) >= _BOARD_PAGE:
        # `/blockers` orders by raised_at ASCENDING and then truncates, so a full page is
        # the OLDEST rows and every row raised today can sit beyond it. Left unsaid, this
        # row would count zero fresh rows against a real escalation and report the
        # producer severed — the precise false alarm it exists to make trustworthy. An
        # honest `unknown` is the only verdict available until the endpoint can be asked
        # for a window rather than a page.
        return Check("escalations", host.base_url or "-",
                     f"the board returned a full page of {_BOARD_PAGE} blocker(s) and it "
                     "pages oldest-first, so rows raised in the last "
                     f"{span} can lie beyond it — this row cannot tell a severed producer "
                     "from a long table", "unknown",
                     manual="give /blockers a raised-after filter or a newest-first order, "
                            "then count the window instead of the page",
                     extra={"posts": len(raised), "page": len(stored),
                            "window_minutes": window})
    fresh = [b for b in stored
             if isinstance(b, dict)
             and (age := _age_minutes(b.get("raised_at"))) is not None
             and 0 <= age <= window]
    if raised and not fresh:
        return Check("escalations", host.base_url or "-",
                     f"{len(raised)} {ESCALATION_POST} post(s) in the last {span} and not "
                     "one blocker row — the escalation path is ringing the bell and "
                     "recording nothing, so nothing can answer 'what is waiting on me'",
                     "fail",
                     manual="run one escalation by hand and read the note it returns: "
                            "`announce()` states which half failed on the line it returns "
                            "(harness/loops/needs_human.py)",
                     extra={"posts": len(raised), "rows": 0, "window_minutes": window})
    if not raised and not fresh:
        return Check("escalations", host.base_url or "-",
                     f"nothing escalated in the last {span}, so an empty blockers table is "
                     "the state of a quiet day and not evidence of a severed producer",
                     "ok", extra={"posts": 0, "rows": 0, "window_minutes": window})
    # Rows and no posts is not a fault either: a person can raise one by hand, and the
    # window is a day while an unanswered blocker outlives it.
    return Check("escalations", host.base_url or "-",
                 f"{len(fresh)} blocker row(s) recorded against {len(raised)} "
                 f"{ESCALATION_POST} post(s) in the last {span}", "ok",
                 extra={"posts": len(raised), "rows": len(fresh), "window_minutes": window})


def check_generated(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Is any open pull request editing a file the release job GENERATES? (#122)

    `CHANGELOG.md` is output. The release number is applied on the integration branch
    after the merge, and a branch that edits it is refused by the pre-push hook and by CI
    — but a guard that only exists in a hook is absent on the machine that needed it
    (#169, five instances in two days), and a pull request opened from a checkout with no
    hooks reaches this state and sits in it.

    The cost is not abstract. Every stamped branch writes the same lines of the same file,
    so N of them in flight is N-choose-2 conflicts BY CONSTRUCTION, over nothing — both
    entries are right and both belong, and git cannot know that two insertions at one
    offset are independent. On 2026-08-23 the three open pull requests that had written a
    release entry were all `CONFLICTING` and the three that had not were all `MERGEABLE`;
    PR #398 landed both ways and settles it.

    ASKED BEFORE THE CONFLICT, not after. #407 proposed this as "are open pull requests
    conflicting on CHANGELOG.md", which is the same fault observed one step later — the
    edit is the fault and the conflict is only its commonest consequence, and a row that
    waits for the conflict cannot fire on the first branch to make it. The conflict count
    is reported beside the finding because it says how urgent it is, not because it is the
    test.

    TWO THINGS IT DOES NOT SEE, both stated because a limit nobody wrote down is a limit
    nobody remembers:

    * **The README's release list**, which the same guard covers. The guard exempts the
      rest of `README.md` so that documenting anything is not taxed, and a list of changed
      paths cannot tell an edit to the release list from an edit to the installation
      instructions. A row that named `README.md` would fail a branch for improving its own
      docs, which is how a row gets ignored.
    * **A rename AWAY from `CHANGELOG.md`.** GitHub reports the resulting path, so a
      branch that renamed the generated file out of the way looks like a branch that
      touched some other file. That is a different and much rarer fault than writing an
      entry into it, and catching it would need the rename metadata this call does not
      carry.
    """
    if not fragments_in_use(host):
        return Check("generated", str(host.repo),
                     "this repo has no changelog.d/, so its release notes are not generated "
                     "output and a branch editing them is not a fault", "ok")
    prs, why = open_pull_requests(host)
    if prs is None:
        return Check("generated", str(host.repo), why, "unknown",
                     manual="see the tools row for gh")
    offenders = [p for p in prs
                 if any(f.get("path") in GENERATED_BY_THE_RELEASE_JOB
                        for f in (p.get("files") or []))]
    # The finding first. Truncation cannot make a pull request that edits CHANGELOG.md
    # stop editing it, so a fail among the ones that WERE read is a fail either way.
    if offenders:
        dirty = [p["number"] for p in offenders if p.get("mergeStateStatus") == "DIRTY"]
        tail = f"; {len(dirty)} of them already conflicting" if dirty else ""
        return Check("generated", str(host.repo),
                     f"{_spell_prs([p['number'] for p in offenders])} edit "
                     f"{', '.join(GENERATED_BY_THE_RELEASE_JOB)}, which the release job "
                     f"writes and no branch does{tail}", "fail",
                     manual="each of those writes `changelog.d/<issue>.<kind>.md` instead "
                            "and reverts the entry — see changelog.d/README.md",
                     extra={"open": len(prs), "offenders": [p["number"] for p in offenders],
                            "conflicting": dirty})
    if truncated(prs):
        # Codex found this as a false green. The cap was named in the detail and the
        # verdict was `ok` anyway, which is a disclosure making an unmade check sound
        # careful — the 51st pull request is exactly where the offender would be.
        return Check("generated", str(host.repo),
                     f"this read the first {PR_SAMPLE} open pull requests and stopped, so "
                     f"whether any beyond them edits {', '.join(GENERATED_BY_THE_RELEASE_JOB)}"
                     " could not be established", "unknown",
                     manual=f"gh pr list --repo {github_slug(host)} --state open "
                            "--limit 1000 --json number,files")
    return Check("generated", str(host.repo),
                 f"none of {len(prs)} open pull request(s) edits "
                 f"{', '.join(GENERATED_BY_THE_RELEASE_JOB)}", "ok",
                 extra={"open": len(prs), "offenders": []})


def check_stamper(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Can a branch in THIS REPO still write a version number itself? (#122)

    #122 deleted `release_stamp.py` rather than documenting against it, because a branch
    that can stamp itself will — and every mechanism that existed only to catch a branch
    that had (the collision check, the push-time reservation, `no unstamped release on
    main`) went with it. If the tool comes back, all of them are missing.

    Its own row rather than half of a stamping one, and Codex is why: a stamper in the
    repository and a stale brief on this machine are two reasons to fire, with two owners
    and two remedies — the code even chose a different `manual` depending on which had
    happened. One row with two premises is exactly how the `merges` row drifted, and
    writing a second one is cheaper than explaining the first.
    """
    if not fragments_in_use(host):
        return Check("stamper", str(host.repo),
                     "this repo has no changelog.d/, so there is no branch-side stamping "
                     "here to have removed", "ok")
    stamper, why = _branch_side_stamper(host)
    if why:
        return Check("stamper", str(host.repo), why, "unknown",
                     manual=f"git -C {host.repo} config --get qb.releaseStamp")
    if stamper is not None:
        return Check("stamper", _relative(stamper, host.repo),
                     f"{_relative(stamper, host.repo)} is a branch-side stamper — a branch "
                     "that can write a version number will, and every guard that existed to "
                     "catch one that had was deleted with it (#122)", "fail",
                     manual="delete it; the number is applied on the integration branch "
                            "after the merge, by scripts/release.py run")
    return Check("stamper", str(host.repo),
                 "nothing here lets a branch write a version number", "ok")


def check_briefs(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Do the briefs THIS HOST WOULD OPEN still tell an agent to stamp a release? (#122)

    The site that is not in the repository, and the reason this is a doctor row and not
    only a test. Five agents stamped on the night of 2026-08-23 and every one of them was
    following a document — *a mechanism removed from the code and left in the brief has
    not been removed*. And the briefs on a host are the ones the harness ON PATH ships,
    which is a different set from the repository's the moment that harness falls behind.
    `test_no_brief_tells_an_agent_to_stamp_a_release` pins what this repo SHIPS; this asks
    what this box READS, and on the machine it was written on the two disagreed.

    Code blocks only, the same line that test draws: prose may explain what used to happen
    and why it does not any more, and a fence may not carry the command. Inside a fence the
    text goes through :func:`_shell_code`, so a commented-out command and a heredoc body
    are not instructions — a false FAIL here sends somebody to rebuild a harness that was
    fine. Quoted spans are deliberately kept, which is the opposite of what the hook
    reading does: briefs quote the paths they run
    (`python3 "$WT_DIR/scripts/release_stamp.py" preflight`), and emptying quoted spans
    here stopped this seeing the very line it exists for.

    A brief this cannot READ is not a brief that read clean. Codex found that: the walk
    skipped an unreadable file and the row went green on the ones that were left.
    """
    if not fragments_in_use(host):
        return Check("briefs", str(host.repo),
                     "this repo has no changelog.d/, so no brief here can tell an agent to "
                     "stop doing something it never did", "ok")
    harness = installed_harness(host)
    briefs = commands_dir(harness)
    if briefs is None:
        return Check("briefs", str(harness),
                     "this harness has no commands/ directory, so the briefs an agent on "
                     "this host reads could not be found — and a stamping instruction left "
                     "in one is how the mechanism comes back", "unknown",
                     manual="reinstall the harness (qb-bump), then re-run")
    telling, unreadable = _briefs_that_tell_an_agent_to_stamp(briefs)
    if telling:
        return Check("briefs", str(briefs),
                     f"{', '.join(sorted(telling))} run a release command #122 removed, in a "
                     "code block — a branch names no version and cuts no release", "fail",
                     manual=f"qb-bump --host $(hostname)   # if {briefs} is a stale harness; "
                            "otherwise the brief itself is wrong",
                     extra={"telling": sorted(telling), "unreadable": sorted(unreadable)})
    if unreadable:
        return Check("briefs", str(briefs),
                     f"{', '.join(sorted(unreadable))} could not be read, so what {'it tells' if len(unreadable) == 1 else 'they tell'} "
                     "an agent to do is not known", "unknown",
                     manual=f"ls -l {briefs}", extra={"unreadable": sorted(unreadable)})
    read = len(list(briefs.glob("*.md")))
    return Check("briefs", str(briefs),
                 f"none of the {read} brief(s) this host reads runs a stamping command in a "
                 "code block", "ok", extra={"read": read})


def _branch_side_stamper(host: Host) -> tuple[Path | None, str | None]:
    """The tool a branch would stamp itself with, or why this cannot say.

    `QB_RELEASE_STAMP`, then `qb.releaseStamp`, then `scripts/release_stamp.py` — the shape
    `release_tagger` uses, so a repo that moved its tools is described by one rule and not
    by two that agree until somebody moves one. And the same three-way answer, for the same
    reason: a DECLARED stamper that is not at the path it was declared at is not an absent
    affordance, and a `git config` that could not be read is not a key that is unset.
    """
    from_env = os.environ.get("QB_RELEASE_STAMP")
    if from_env:
        return ((Path(from_env), None) if Path(from_env).is_file() else
                (None, f"QB_RELEASE_STAMP names {from_env}, which is not a file — so what "
                       "a branch here could stamp with is declared and not there"))
    rc, cfg, err = run_cmd(["git", "-C", str(host.repo), "config", "--get", "qb.releaseStamp"])
    if rc not in (0, 1):
        return None, ("this checkout will not say what `qb.releaseStamp` is set to "
                      f"({clip_line(err) or f'git config exit {rc}'})")
    if rc == 0 and cfg:
        cand = Path(cfg) if cfg.startswith("/") else host.repo / cfg
        return ((cand, None) if cand.is_file() else
                (None, f"qb.releaseStamp names {cfg}, which is not a file — so what a branch "
                       "here could stamp with is declared and not there"))
    default = host.repo / "scripts" / "release_stamp.py"
    return (default if default.is_file() else None), None


def _briefs_that_tell_an_agent_to_stamp(briefs: Path) -> tuple[set[str], set[str]]:
    """``(the briefs that do, the briefs that could not be read)``."""
    telling: set[str] = set()
    unreadable: set[str] = set()
    for path in sorted(briefs.glob("*.md")):
        text = _read(path)
        if text is None:
            unreadable.add(path.name)
            continue
        runnable = _shell_code(fenced(text))
        if any(re.search(pattern, runnable) for pattern in _STAMPING_COMMANDS):
            telling.add(path.name)
    return telling, unreadable


def fenced(text: str) -> str:
    """Every fenced code block in a markdown document, concatenated.

    A scanner rather than a regex, because a regex over ``` gets three things wrong and
    Codex named all three: a `~~~` fence is missed entirely, a four-backtick fence is
    closed by the first ``` inside it, and the closer's indentation is not the opener's.
    The rule here is CommonMark's: a fence opens with three or more `` ` `` or `~`, and is
    closed by a run of the SAME character at least as long, with nothing after it.

    Indented fences are the reason any of this matters at all — every fence in
    `fix-and-review.md` sits inside a numbered step, so an anchored ``` matches nothing,
    and "no forbidden command in any code block" over zero code blocks passes on every
    possible file.
    """
    out: list[str] = []
    opener: tuple[str, int] | None = None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        char = stripped[:1]
        if char in ("`", "~"):
            run = len(stripped) - len(stripped.lstrip(char))
            rest = stripped[run:]
            if opener is None:
                if run >= 3 and (char != "`" or "`" not in rest):
                    opener = (char, run)
                    continue
            elif char == opener[0] and run >= opener[1] and not rest:
                opener = None
                continue
        if opener is not None:
            out.append(line)
    return "\n".join(out)


#: What a tag reconciliation has to state before this will read it as one. Every key,
#: with the right container type — because a report that omitted them all would otherwise
#: default to four empty collections and be reported as a clean repository. Codex found
#: that: `{}`, `{"clean": false}`, and any unrelated program that happens to print JSON
#: all came out `ok`.
_TAG_REPORT = {"orphaned": dict, "reserved": dict, "misplaced": dict, "untagged": list}

#: The only two exit codes a reconciliation may have. `release_tag.py` documents 0 = clean,
#: 1 = a condition could not be checked, 2 = STOP — so anything else is a program that did
#: not answer this question, whatever it printed.
_TAG_EXITS = (0, 2)


def check_tags(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Do the release tags point into the history they claim to tag?

    #406's other half. `v3.8` was tagged at a commit a squash merge had discarded, so the
    tag addressed history nobody could reach while the v3.8 entry sat on `main` — and the
    CI job named `every release on main has a tag` stayed green, because it asked whether
    a tag of that NAME resolved. CI only runs on a push; a person runs the doctor when
    something feels wrong, which is when this gets asked.

    ONE ROW, because there is one invariant: *a tag `vX.Y` points at a commit whose
    CHANGELOG declares `vX.Y`*. That is `release_tag.py`'s own single sentence, and
    `untagged`, `misplaced` and `orphaned` are the three ways it can be false, not three
    separate questions. The tool reports one condition per line for the reader; the row
    reports one verdict about the invariant, and leads with the orphan because that is the
    one a merge strategy caused.

    A LIVE RESERVATION IS NOT A FINDING, and telling it from an orphan is the whole
    difficulty. A tag off the integration ref is two things wearing one face: a release
    that has not landed there (a local cut not yet pushed, or a pre-#122 reservation),
    which holds a number nothing else will hand out and is fine; or a release that HAS
    landed and whose tag is somewhere else, which is the defect. One
    `git merge-base --is-ancestor` cannot make that call — it needs the CHANGELOG at the
    ref to say which releases have landed. Reporting reservations would train people to
    ignore the row, which is worse than not having it.

    THIS ROW EXECUTES A PROGRAM OUT OF THE REPOSITORY, and that is a decision rather than
    an oversight. `merges` reads its tagger and refuses to run it, because `merges` asks
    what a tool IS and running a program to find that out is the hazard itself. This asks
    what the repository's TAGS ARE, and the tagger is the only thing that can answer: what
    counts as a release heading here is a masked-markdown question with fenced blocks,
    inline code spans and blockquoted fences all mattering, and this repo's CHANGELOG
    quotes `## vX.Y` inside examples. `release_tag.py`'s own `_load_stamper` refuses to
    keep a second copy of that rule, and a second copy here would agree with it until the
    morning one of them was asked about a squash.

    The parse that precedes the run is a COMPATIBILITY GATE and not a sandbox, and Codex
    was right to say so: module-level code runs before any argument is parsed, and the
    file can change between the read and the exec. What the gate buys is that an old or
    foreign tagger is not invoked with a subcommand it does not have; what makes running
    it acceptable is that this is the repository's own release tool, which its pre-push
    hook and its CI already run on every push. What is NOT trusted is the answer: the exit
    code, every field and its type are all checked before a verdict is reached.
    """
    tagger, why = release_tagger(host)
    if why:
        return Check("tags", str(host.repo), why, "unknown",
                     manual=f"git -C {host.repo} config --get qb.releaseTag")
    if tagger is None:
        return Check("tags", str(host.repo),
                     "this repo has no release tagger, so nothing here can reconcile a tag "
                     "against the history it names", "ok")
    rel = _relative(tagger, host.repo)
    text = _read(tagger)
    names = _python_subcommands(text) if text is not None and _is_python(tagger, text) else None
    if names is None or "check" not in names:
        return Check("tags", rel,
                     f"{rel} does not declare a `check` subcommand this could read, and "
                     "nothing else here can tell a tag left over from a reservation from one "
                     "a squash merge orphaned", "unknown",
                     manual=f"{rel} check --json --repo {host.repo}")
    base = _default_branch(host)
    if base is None:
        # NOT a fallback to HEAD. The question is about the ref work lands on, and asking
        # it of whatever branch this worktree happens to be on would answer a different
        # question in the same words.
        return Check("tags", str(host.repo),
                     f"could not resolve {host.remote}'s default branch, and a tag is "
                     "orphaned or reserved only with respect to the ref work lands on",
                     "unknown",
                     manual=f"git -C {host.repo} remote set-head {host.remote} --auto")
    ref = f"{host.remote}/{base}"
    argv = [sys.executable, str(tagger), "check", "--json", "--repo", str(host.repo),
            "--ref", ref]
    rc, out, err = run_cmd(argv, cwd=str(host.repo), timeout=60)
    report, complaint = _tag_report(rc, out)
    if report is None:
        return Check("tags", ref, f"{rel} {complaint}"
                     + (f": {clip_line(err)}" if err else ""), "unknown",
                     manual=" ".join(argv[1:]))
    orphaned, untagged = report["orphaned"], report["untagged"]
    misplaced, reserved = report["misplaced"], report["reserved"]
    # Said in every verdict, including the passing one, because a reader who does not know
    # that reservations are excluded cannot tell a quiet row from an incurious one.
    aside = (f"; {len(reserved)} tag(s) off {ref} are unpushed local cuts or pre-#122 "
             "reservations, which is not a finding" if reserved else "")
    if orphaned:
        return Check("tags", ref,
                     f"{', '.join(sorted(orphaned))} landed on {ref} and "
                     f"{'is' if len(orphaned) == 1 else 'are'} tagged off it — a rewriting "
                     f"merge discarded the commit the tag named (#406){aside}", "fail",
                     manual=f"{rel} check --ref {ref}   # it prints where each one landed and "
                            "the leased force-push to re-point it; nothing moves a tag for you",
                     extra={"orphaned": orphaned, "reserved": reserved})
    if untagged or misplaced:
        parts = []
        if untagged:
            parts.append(f"{', '.join(sorted(untagged))} shipped on {ref} with no tag")
        if misplaced:
            parts.append(f"{', '.join(sorted(misplaced))} tagged at a commit that does not "
                         "declare it")
        return Check("tags", ref, "; ".join(parts) + aside, "fail",
                     manual=f"{rel} check --ref {ref}",
                     extra={"untagged": untagged, "misplaced": misplaced,
                            "reserved": reserved})
    return Check("tags", ref, f"every release on {ref} is tagged, and every tag is on the "
                 f"history it names{aside}", "ok", extra={"reserved": reserved})


def _tag_report(rc: int, out: str) -> tuple[dict | None, str]:
    """A reconciliation this row is willing to read a verdict out of, or why it is not.

    Nothing here is inferred from what is missing. Every key must be present with the
    right container type, the exit code must be one of the two this question has, and the
    two must agree — a `0` alongside findings, or a `2` alongside none, is a program whose
    answer this cannot use, whichever half were believed.
    """
    if rc not in _TAG_EXITS:
        return None, f"exited {rc}, which is not an answer about the tags"
    try:
        report = json.loads(out)
    except ValueError:
        return None, "did not answer a report about the tags"
    if not isinstance(report, dict):
        return None, "did not answer a report about the tags"
    for key, kind in _TAG_REPORT.items():
        if not isinstance(report.get(key), kind):
            return None, f"answered a report with no usable `{key}`"
    findings = any(report[k] for k in ("orphaned", "untagged", "misplaced"))
    if findings != (rc == 2):
        return None, (f"exited {rc} and reported "
                      f"{'findings' if findings else 'none'}, which disagree")
    return report, ""


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# the semantic group — the questions a predicate cannot decide (#408)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
#
# THE RULE THIS GROUP EXISTS UNDER: Python first, and Python wherever a predicate
# can decide it. An LLM check that duplicates a predicate is strictly worse —
# slower, costlier, non-deterministic — so the only questions here are the ones
# no predicate can answer. `briefs` reads FENCED BLOCKS, deterministically, and
# deliberately lets prose alone, because prose explaining that a mechanism was
# removed is the removal working. Whether a paragraph DIRECTS a worker or merely
# DESCRIBES what used to happen is a question about meaning, and it is the exact
# question that would have prevented the night of 2026-08-23, when five agents
# stamped a release because a document told them to.
#
# AND THE RULE THAT KEEPS IT HONEST: an LLM check must be able to answer
# `unknown`, and must do so by default when it cannot see enough. That is
# enforced in THREE places in Python, only one of which is prompt design, because
# a model that has seen nothing will still answer confidently:
#
#   1. :func:`gather_evidence` — the manifest, decided before the call. Nothing
#      readable, one file unreadable, or more bytes than the ceiling allows, and
#      the row is `unknown` WITHOUT the model having been asked. This is the
#      `edge` row's shape: "nothing here can see whether the secret is set" is
#      decided by the code, not by the thing being asked.
#   2. :func:`ask_model` — the wrapper. No CLI, disabled, timed out, non-zero
#      exit, no JSON, a verdict outside the closed vocabulary, or an answer citing
#      a file it was not given or quoting a line that is not in the text. One
#      place where "could not be asked" is phrased, so no row can reach a verdict
#      without having been answered — the same shape `board_get` and
#      `open_pull_requests` already have.
#   3. The prompt, which offers `cannot tell` and says it is never a polite way of
#      agreeing. Weakest of the three on its own, which is why it is third.
#
# AND IT IS BOUNDED, three ways. It does not run by default — `SCHEDULED` keeps
# the group out of a bare `qb-doctor`, because this is a command people type when
# something already feels wrong and unbounded spend attached to that is #55's
# argument. Every call carries a dollar ceiling the CLI itself enforces and a
# timeout. And the answer is CACHED ON THE DIGEST OF WHAT WAS READ, so a
# scheduled re-run over unchanged documents costs nothing at all — which is how
# the expensive half stays affordable without being opt-in-and-therefore-never-run.

#: Which groups a bare `qb-doctor` leaves out. Selection IS the bound: `--only
#: semantic` runs them, `--only landing,semantic` puts them on the timer beside the
#: rows that already run there, and typing `qb-doctor` costs what it always did.
SCHEDULED = ("semantic",)

#: The CLI a semantic check asks. A subprocess to a vendor CLI rather than an SDK,
#: because that is how everything else in this harness asks a model (`panel_seats`,
#: `epic.triage`) and adding an SDK dependency to a diagnostic that must run on a
#: host too stale to have `qb-bump` would be the wrong trade.
LLM_CLI = "claude"

#: Off switch, for the two places that need one: a sandbox with no network (this
#: suite's own nix check runs there) and a host whose owner does not want model
#: calls made from it. `0`, `off`, `no` and `false` all mean off.
LLM_ENV = "QB_DOCTOR_LLM"

#: Model and per-call ceiling. The ceiling is enforced by the CLI (`--max-budget-usd`)
#: rather than by an estimate here, so it holds even if this file is wrong about what
#: a call costs. It has to clear the fixed overhead of a session as well as the
#: evidence, which is why it is not a few cents.
LLM_MODEL = "sonnet"
LLM_BUDGET_USD = "1.00"
LLM_TIMEOUT = 240

#: The most model calls one run of one row may make. The byte ceiling bounds how much
#: is read; this bounds how often it is asked, and the two are separate because the
#: answer to "one document at a time" is more calls over the same bytes.
LLM_MAX_CALLS = 40

#: The evidence ceiling, in bytes. Reaching it is an `unknown` and NOT a disclosed
#: pass — #417 closed exactly that hole in the GitHub-backed rows, where a truncated
#: read said so in its detail and returned `ok` anyway. A corpus this could not read
#: whole is a corpus it cannot clear.
LLM_MAX_BYTES = 400_000

#: Tools the model is refused. It is given every document it may look at and asked
#: about those; anything it went and read on its own would be evidence this row
#: cannot show a reader, and the answer validation would reject a quote from it.
LLM_NO_TOOLS = ("Bash", "Read", "Glob", "Grep", "WebFetch", "WebSearch", "Edit",
                "Write", "Task", "NotebookEdit")

#: The shortest a quotation may be and still be evidence. Codex found the hole: a
#: `telling` answer citing `"a"` satisfies "the quote is in the file" for every file
#: ever written, so the citation check would pass while establishing nothing. A
#: sentence a worker could act on is longer than this; anything shorter is not the
#: instruction it claims to have found.
MIN_QUOTE = 24

#: How long a cached answer stands. The evidence digest is already in the key, so
#: this is not for correctness — it is so a wrong verdict ages out and the file
#: does not grow without bound.
LLM_CACHE_DAYS = 7
LLM_CACHE_MAX = 200


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Evidence:
    """What a semantic check may read, decided in Python before anything is asked.

    ``why`` is the whole point of the type: it is empty when the manifest is usable
    and a sentence when it is not, and a caller that reaches the model with a
    non-empty ``why`` is a bug. `files` is `(name, text)` so that an answer citing a
    file can be checked against the text that was actually supplied, rather than
    against whatever is on disk by the time somebody reads the report.
    """

    files: tuple[tuple[str, str], ...] = ()
    digest: str = ""
    why: str = ""

    @property
    def names(self) -> list[str]:
        return [name for name, _text in self.files]

    @property
    def size(self) -> int:
        return sum(len(text) for _name, text in self.files)

    def each(self) -> "list[Evidence]":
        """This manifest as one Evidence per document — the unit a question is asked in.

        A model asked to find one sentence in thirteen documents at once answered `clean`
        about a corpus that visibly contained it, on the second run, over the same bytes
        it had answered `telling` about on the first. That is the failure #408 names in
        as many words: an LLM check returning the benign answer because it found nothing.
        A needle in one document is a question a model reliably answers; a needle in a
        corpus is a question it plausibly declines to.

        It costs no more to ask this way — the same bytes are read either way and only
        the per-call overhead multiplies — and it buys two things beside reliability. A
        finding is scoped to the document it is in, so the citation check is over the one
        file it could have come from. And the cache is per document, so editing one brief
        re-asks about one brief.
        """
        return [Evidence(files=(one,), digest=_digest((one,))) for one in self.files]


def gather_evidence(paths: list[Path], transform=None) -> Evidence:
    """The manifest, or why there is not one. Never partial, never truncated.

    A file that cannot be read makes the whole manifest unusable rather than being
    skipped, which is the correction #417 made to `check_briefs` for the same
    reason: the walk that skips the unreadable file and passes on the rest is
    reporting a clean corpus it never read.
    """
    if not paths:
        return Evidence(why="there is nothing here to read")
    out: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
    for path in sorted(paths):
        text = _read(path)
        if text is None:
            return Evidence(why=f"{path.name} could not be read, so the set this would "
                                "have judged is not the set that is there")
        out.append((path.name, transform(text) if transform else text))
    size = sum(len(t) for _n, t in out)
    if size > LLM_MAX_BYTES:
        return Evidence(why=f"{len(out)} file(s) come to {size} bytes, over the "
                            f"{LLM_MAX_BYTES}-byte ceiling — a corpus this could not read "
                            "whole is one it cannot clear")
    return Evidence(files=tuple(out), digest=_digest(tuple(out)))


def _digest(files: tuple[tuple[str, str], ...]) -> str:
    joined = "\0".join(f"{n}\0{t}" for n, t in files)
    return hashlib.sha256(joined.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]


def llm_enabled() -> bool:
    return os.environ.get(LLM_ENV, "1").strip().lower() not in ("0", "off", "no", "false")


def _cache_path() -> Path:
    root = os.environ.get("XDG_CACHE_HOME") or str(Path.home() / ".cache")
    return Path(root) / "quarterback" / "doctor-llm.json"


def _cache_key(row: str, question: str, evidence: Evidence, sample: int = 1) -> str:
    """Row, question, model, and the bytes that were read — all four, none by discipline.

    The question is hashed in rather than tracked by a version constant somebody has to
    remember to bump: Codex pointed out that a reworded question would otherwise read
    yesterday's answer to a different one, and a rule enforced by remembering is not one.
    """
    asked = hashlib.sha256(question.encode()).hexdigest()[:12]
    return f"{row}:{asked}:{LLM_MODEL}:{evidence.digest}:{sample}"


def cache_get(key: str) -> dict | None:
    """Yesterday's answer to exactly this question about exactly these bytes.

    Best-effort in both directions: an unreadable or corrupt cache is a cache miss,
    never an error, because a diagnostic that fell over on its own scratch file
    would be unavailable precisely when somebody needed it.
    """
    try:
        held = json.loads(_cache_path().read_text())
        entry = held[key]
        age = datetime.now(UTC) - datetime.fromisoformat(entry["at"])
    except Exception:                                         # noqa: BLE001 — a miss
        return None
    if age.days >= LLM_CACHE_DAYS:
        return None
    answer = entry.get("answer")
    return answer if isinstance(answer, dict) else None


def cache_put(key: str, answer: dict) -> None:
    path = _cache_path()
    try:
        loaded = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except Exception:                                         # noqa: BLE001 — start fresh
        loaded = {}
    # Entries that are not dicts are dropped ON LOAD rather than tripped over during
    # eviction, which is where Codex found them raising: `kv[1].get("at")` on a corrupt
    # file with more than LLM_CACHE_MAX entries is an AttributeError outside the guard.
    held = {k: v for k, v in loaded.items() if isinstance(v, dict)} \
        if isinstance(loaded, dict) else {}
    held[key] = {"at": datetime.now(UTC).isoformat(), "answer": answer}
    if len(held) > LLM_CACHE_MAX:
        oldest = sorted(held.items(), key=lambda kv: str(kv[1].get("at")))
        held = dict(oldest[-LLM_CACHE_MAX:])
    with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        path.write_text(json.dumps(held, indent=1, sort_keys=True))


#: The three answers a semantic question may come back with. Closed, for the reason
#: `VERDICTS` is closed: a fourth invented by the model has to become an `unknown`
#: rather than something the caller pattern-matches its way past.
_ANSWERS = ("clean", "telling", "cannot tell")


def _prompt(question: str, evidence: Evidence) -> str:
    parts = [question, "", f"THE {len(evidence.files)} DOCUMENT(S) YOU MAY JUDGE, and "
             "nothing else exists for this question:"]
    for name, text in evidence.files:
        parts += ["", f"===== BEGIN {name} =====", text, f"===== END {name} ====="]
    return "\n".join(parts)


def ask_model(row: str, question: str, evidence: Evidence,
              sample: int = 1) -> tuple[dict | None, str]:
    """``(answer, why-there-is-none)``. The one place "could not be asked" is phrased.

    Everything that can go wrong between deciding to ask and having an answer comes
    out of here as a sentence, so no check below can reach a verdict without having
    been answered. That is the same contract `board_get` and `open_pull_requests`
    hold for the GitHub-backed rows, and the group-wide meta-test asserts it the same
    way.

    The last two clauses are the ones that matter and the ones a prompt cannot
    enforce. A `telling` verdict must name a file FROM THE MANIFEST and quote text
    that is actually IN it — an answer that cites something it was not given is a
    model reasoning about its own memory of this repository, and a quote that is not
    in the text is one it composed. Both are discarded rather than believed, and
    discarding them is an `unknown`, never a `clean`: the failure this whole tool
    exists to catch is the absent signal rendering as the benign one.
    """
    if evidence.why:
        return None, evidence.why
    if not llm_enabled():
        return None, f"model checks are switched off here ({LLM_ENV})"
    if shutil.which(LLM_CLI) is None:
        return None, f"no {LLM_CLI} on this host, so nothing here can ask the question"
    key = _cache_key(row, question, evidence, sample)
    cached = cache_get(key)
    if cached is not None:
        answer, why = validated(cached, evidence)
        if answer is None:
            return None, f"a cached answer no longer stands up: {why}"
        return {**answer, "cached": True}, ""
    argv = [LLM_CLI, "-p", "--model", LLM_MODEL, "--max-budget-usd", LLM_BUDGET_USD,
            "--disallowed-tools", *LLM_NO_TOOLS]
    rc, out, err = run_cmd(argv, timeout=LLM_TIMEOUT,
                           stdin=_prompt(question, evidence))
    if rc != 0:
        return None, f"{LLM_CLI} exited {rc}: {clip_line(err or out) or 'and said nothing'}"
    # The CLI reports a reached budget on stdout with exit 0, which `panel_seats`
    # also has to special-case. An answer that was never generated is not an answer.
    if "budget" in out.lower() and "exceed" in out.lower():
        return None, f"the ${LLM_BUDGET_USD} ceiling for one call was reached: {clip_line(out)}"
    match = re.search(r"\{.*\}", out, re.S)
    if not match:
        return None, f"{LLM_CLI} answered with no JSON object: {clip_line(out) or '(nothing)'}"
    try:
        parsed = json.loads(match.group(0))
    except ValueError:
        return None, f"{LLM_CLI}'s answer did not parse: {clip_line(match.group(0))}"
    answer, why = validated(parsed, evidence)
    if answer is None:
        return None, why
    cache_put(key, answer)
    return {**answer, "cached": False}, ""


def validated(answer: object, evidence: Evidence) -> tuple[dict | None, str]:
    """Everything an answer must satisfy — applied to a CACHED one as well as a fresh one.

    Codex found the hole this closes, and it is the family this file exists to keep out:
    a cached answer that skipped these checks reaches `check_instructions`, is not
    `telling`, and falls through to the `ok` return. A hand-edited or corrupt cache file
    would therefore have been a way to make a green row, which is the absent signal
    rendering as the benign one arriving through the back door of an optimisation.

    So validation is not part of parsing; it is a function of the answer and the evidence,
    and both routes to an answer go through it.
    """
    if not isinstance(answer, dict) or answer.get("verdict") not in _ANSWERS:
        got = answer.get("verdict") if isinstance(answer, dict) else answer
        return None, (f"the answer's verdict was {got!r}, which is not one of {_ANSWERS}")
    if answer["verdict"] == "cannot tell":
        return None, f"the model could not tell: {clip_line(str(answer.get('reason', '')))}"
    if answer["verdict"] == "telling":
        named, quote = str(answer.get("file", "")), str(answer.get("quote", ""))
        if named not in evidence.names:
            return None, (f"the answer cited {named!r}, which was not among the documents "
                          "it was given — so it is about something this cannot show you")
        tidy = " ".join(quote.split())
        if len(tidy) < MIN_QUOTE:
            return None, (f"the answer quoted {tidy!r} from {named}, which is too short to "
                          f"be the instruction it claims to have found ({MIN_QUOTE} "
                          "characters is the floor) — a substring is not a citation")
        if tidy not in " ".join(dict(evidence.files)[named].split()):
            return None, (f"the answer quoted a line that is not in {named} — a composed "
                          "quotation is not evidence, and this is an unknown rather than a "
                          "finding")
    return answer, ""


def unfenced(text: str) -> str:
    """A markdown document with every fenced code block removed — its PROSE.

    The complement of :func:`fenced`, and the split that keeps this group from
    duplicating a predicate: `briefs` reads what is inside the fences and this reads
    what is outside them, so the two cannot disagree about the same line and the
    model is never billed for text a `grep` already settled.
    """
    out: list[str] = []
    opener: tuple[str, int] | None = None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        char = stripped[:1]
        if char in ("`", "~"):
            run = len(stripped) - len(stripped.lstrip(char))
            rest = stripped[run:]
            if opener is None:
                if run >= 3 and (char != "`" or "`" not in rest):
                    opener = (char, run)
                    continue
            elif char == opener[0] and run >= opener[1] and not rest:
                opener = None
                continue
        if opener is None:
            out.append(line)
    return "\n".join(out)


#: The question, and every word of it is load-bearing — this is the ONE piece of the
#: honest-unknown that lives in the prompt rather than in Python, and it is third of
#: three because it is the weakest.
#:
#: The clause that earns its place is "if you cannot tell whether a passage orders or
#: describes, that is `cannot tell`". #408's requirement is that the check default to
#: unknown when it cannot see enough, and the first cut read that as being about the
#: amount of TEXT — so a passage the model found genuinely ambiguous came back `clean`,
#: which is the benign default arriving by the only route left open to it. Not seeing
#: enough and not being able to decide are the same failure to establish something, and
#: they get the same answer.
STAMPING_QUESTION = """You are auditing one agent-facing instruction document for one
removed mechanism.

BACKGROUND, which is settled fact and not something for you to weigh:
Release version numbers in this project are assigned on the integration branch AFTER a
merge, by a release tool a person runs. Issue #122 removed every branch-side way of doing
it. A branch names no version, reserves no release number, assembles no release entry, and
cuts no release. A worker that produces a version anyway produces a number that cannot be
right, because a branch cannot know which release it will land in.

YOUR QUESTION, and it is only this one:
Does the prose below DIRECT a worker to make a release version number exist on a branch?

That covers being told to name one, stamp one, bump one, reserve one, assemble a release
entry, apply a version to one, or run a release step before the merge. It is the OUTCOME
that matters, not the wording: a passage that tells the worker when to do it, or in what
order, or what to do if it fails, is directing them to do it.

Three things constrain the answer.

PROSE ONLY. The fenced code blocks have been removed before you were given this, because a
separate deterministic check already reads those and this one must not duplicate it. Do
not reason about what a command would do. Reason about what the text tells a worker to do.

INSTRUCTIONS, NOT DESCRIPTIONS. A passage explaining that this USED to happen and no
longer does, or explaining why a branch names no version, is the removal working as
intended, and it is CLEAN.

AND IF YOU CANNOT TELL WHICH OF THOSE A PASSAGE IS, SAY SO. "cannot tell" is a real
answer and it is the right one whenever you would otherwise be guessing — including when
the text is there in front of you and you simply cannot decide whether it orders or
describes. It is never a polite way of saying clean. Failing to see enough and failing to
be able to decide are the same thing here and they get the same answer.

ANSWER with one JSON object and nothing else, no prose around it:
  {"verdict": "clean"}
  {"verdict": "telling", "file": "<the exact filename below>",
   "quote": "<the sentence, copied exactly from the text>"}
  {"verdict": "cannot tell", "reason": "<one line>"}

The quote is checked against the text below and must be at least 24 characters. An answer
whose quote is not found there is discarded rather than believed, so copy it exactly and
do not paraphrase."""


def judge(one: Evidence) -> tuple[str, dict | str, int]:
    """One document, judged — and `clean` has to be said twice.

    THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT, and it is this repo's own rule about absence rather than
    a hedge against a flaky model. A `telling` answer arrives carrying evidence: a
    filename from the manifest and a sentence :func:`validated` has already found in the
    text, so a reader can go and look, and one sample is enough to establish it. A `clean`
    answer carries nothing at all. It is an assertion that something is not there, made by
    a process that cannot show its work — the exact shape of claim this whole file exists
    to distrust, and #408 names it: "an LLM check that returns `ok` because it found
    nothing is the exact failure this tool exists to catch".

    So a single `clean` is not an established absence, and this asks again — a second,
    independent sample under its own cache key, because a cached first answer would
    otherwise confirm itself. That is an argument from what the two answers ARE and not
    from an observed flake: no run on the machine this was written on has yet disagreed
    with itself, and the honest reason for the second reading is that a miss is the one
    outcome this check must not produce, while a false finding is caught by a reader
    following the quote. Paying twice on the path that claims nothing is there is the
    cheap side of that trade.

    It costs nothing on the path that finds something, and `readings` in the row's `extra`
    says how many questions were actually asked.

    Disagreement resolves toward the finding, which is the same order #417 settled for the
    truncated reads: an answer that found nothing cannot make an answer that found
    something stop having found it. Anything else — either sample unanswerable, the model
    unable to tell — is `unknown`, never `clean`.

    Returns the verdict, the answer or the reason there is none, and how many readings
    it took.
    """
    first, why = ask_model("instructions", STAMPING_QUESTION, one, sample=1)
    if first is None:
        return "unknown", why, 1
    if first["verdict"] == "telling":
        return "telling", first, 1
    second, why2 = ask_model("instructions", STAMPING_QUESTION, one, sample=2)
    if second is None:
        return "unknown", (f"one reading found nothing and the second could not be made, "
                           f"so nothing was established: {why2}"), 2
    if second["verdict"] == "telling":
        return "telling", second, 2
    return "clean", "", 2


def check_instructions(host: Host) -> Check:
    """Do the briefs this host reads still TELL a worker to stamp a release, in prose?

    The semantic sibling of `check_briefs`, and deliberately disjoint from it. That row
    reads fenced blocks with a scanner and cannot look at prose, because prose is where
    the removal is explained and a predicate cannot tell an explanation from an
    instruction. This one is handed the prose with the fences taken out and asked exactly
    that question, which is the one that would have prevented the night five agents
    stamped: every one of them was following a document.

    ONE DOCUMENT PER CALL — see :meth:`Evidence.each` for why, and it is not an
    optimisation. Everything else that could make the answer unsafe is decided in Python:
    :func:`gather_evidence` refuses an incomplete manifest before anything is asked, and
    :func:`ask_model` is the only place a verdict can come from.

    THE READ-WHOLE RULE, applied across the calls rather than within one. A document that
    could not be judged makes the row `unknown` even if the others came back clean —
    "twelve of thirteen are fine" is not an answer to "do the briefs on this host tell an
    agent to stamp". But a finding among the ones that WERE judged is still a finding, so
    the fail branch comes first: an unanswerable thirteenth cannot make a telling third
    stop telling.
    """
    if not fragments_in_use(host):
        return Check("instructions", str(host.repo),
                     "this repo has no changelog.d/, so no brief here can tell an agent to "
                     "stop doing something it never did", "ok")
    harness = installed_harness(host)
    briefs = commands_dir(harness)
    if briefs is None:
        return Check("instructions", str(harness),
                     "this harness has no commands/ directory, so the prose an agent on "
                     "this host reads could not be found", "unknown",
                     manual="reinstall the harness (qb-bump), then re-run")
    evidence = gather_evidence(sorted(briefs.glob("*.md")), transform=unfenced)
    seen = {"read": evidence.names, "bytes": evidence.size, "digest": evidence.digest,
            "model": LLM_MODEL}
    if evidence.why:
        return Check("instructions", str(briefs),
                     f"nothing here could judge what the prose in these briefs tells an "
                     f"agent to do: {evidence.why}", "unknown", extra=seen)
    # Two per document, because a `clean` has to be said twice — see :func:`judge`.
    if len(evidence.files) * 2 > LLM_MAX_CALLS:
        return Check("instructions", str(briefs),
                     f"{len(evidence.files)} documents at up to two readings each is more "
                     f"than the {LLM_MAX_CALLS} questions one run of this row may ask, so "
                     "it was not asked at all", "unknown", extra=seen)
    telling: dict[str, str] = {}
    unanswered: list[str] = []
    cached = readings = 0
    for one in evidence.each():
        name = one.names[0]
        verdict, said, asked = judge(one)
        readings += asked
        if verdict == "unknown":
            unanswered.append(f"{name} ({said})")
            continue
        if verdict == "telling":
            telling[name] = str(said.get("quote", ""))
        cached += 1 if isinstance(said, dict) and said.get("cached") else 0
    seen |= {"asked": len(evidence.files), "readings": readings, "cached": cached}
    if telling:
        named = ", ".join(sorted(telling))
        return Check("instructions", str(briefs),
                     f"{named} still tell(s) an agent in prose to stamp or reserve a "
                     "release — #122 removed every branch-side way of doing it, and a brief "
                     "is what an agent does", "fail",
                     manual=f"read {briefs}/{sorted(telling)[0]} and compare it against "
                            f"{host.repo}/harness/commands/{sorted(telling)[0]}",
                     extra={**seen, "telling": sorted(telling), "quote": telling,
                            "unanswered": unanswered})
    if unanswered:
        return Check("instructions", str(briefs),
                     f"{len(unanswered)} of {len(evidence.files)} brief(s) could not be "
                     f"judged, so what the prose on this host tells an agent is not known: "
                     f"{'; '.join(unanswered[:2])}", "unknown",
                     extra={**seen, "unanswered": unanswered})
    return Check("instructions", str(briefs),
                 f"the prose of all {len(evidence.names)} brief(s) this host reads was "
                 "judged, one at a time and twice each, and none of it directs an agent to "
                 "stamp a release", "ok", extra=seen)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# composing the brief — the half #408 is about
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

#: Row facts a brief may name without declaring them in `needs`. They are on every
#: `Check` by construction, so there is nothing for an evidence gate to gate.
_ALWAYS = ("row", "group", "subject", "detail", "verdict", "repo")


def _spell(value: object) -> str:
    """One `extra` value, in a sentence rather than in Python's repr.

    A brief is read by a person or pasted to an agent, and `['a.md', 'b.md']` in the
    middle of an instruction is noise both of them have to parse back.
    """
    if isinstance(value, (list, tuple, set)):
        return ", ".join(str(v) for v in sorted(value, key=str)) or "(none)"
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        return ", ".join(f"{k}={v}" for k, v in sorted(value.items())) or "(none)"
    if value is None:
        return "(none)"
    return str(value)


def _absent(check: Check, needs: tuple[str, ...]) -> list[str]:
    """Which of a brief's required facts this row did not actually establish.

    Absent and *empty* are both absent: `offenders: []` is the row saying it found
    no offenders, and a brief written around which pull requests offend has nothing
    to be written around. `0` is not empty — `waited_minutes: 0` is a measurement.
    """
    out = []
    for key in needs:
        if key not in check.extra:
            out.append(key)
            continue
        value = check.extra[key]
        if value is None or (hasattr(value, "__len__") and len(value) == 0):
            out.append(key)
    return out


def _recheck(check: Check, host: Host) -> str:
    """How to know it worked, for every agent brief: ask the row again.

    Appended by this file rather than written into each registration, because it is
    a command and not an understanding — the one mechanical part of a brief, and the
    part #414 is an issue about. There, `qb-bump`'s own exit code was read as the
    harness having been carried; the answer was that only a re-check can say so, and
    `apply_fixes` already draws the same line for `--fix`.

    Spelled as a path INTO THE CHECKOUT rather than as a bare `qb-doctor`, which is
    #422: the staler a host, the likelier the remedy is missing from it, and the
    checkout is present by definition — this tool is comparing against it.
    """
    return (f"python3 {host.repo}/harness/bin/qb-doctor --only {check.name} "
            f"--repo {host.repo}   # the row itself, re-asked — not the exit code of "
            "whatever you ran")


def compose(spec: CheckSpec, check: Check, host: Host) -> tuple[str | None, str | None, str]:
    """``(prompt, audience, why-there-is-none)`` for one row, as it came out today.

    Three ways this returns no prompt, and the last is the one #408 turns on.

    1. **No brief is registered for this verdict.** The default. An `ok` row has
       nothing to dispatch, and plenty of rows have nothing useful to say about
       their own `warn` either.
    2. **A brief is registered and the row did not establish the facts it is written
       around.** `queue`'s brief is "go and ask whoever holds the head of the line";
       a `queue` row that could not read the queue has no head to name. Rendering it
       anyway produces a confident instruction with a hole in it, which is precisely
       the family #411 files: an unknown rendering as a known one, in the benign
       direction, because the template had to emit *something*.
    3. **The template names a fact nothing supplies.** A typo in a registration is a
       missing brief, not a brief containing `{holder}`.

    In all three the answer is a sentence saying so. A caller that got `None` and no
    reason could not tell "nothing to dispatch here" from "the brief broke", and
    those are opposite facts about the same row.
    """
    brief = spec.briefs.get(check.verdict)
    if brief is None:
        return None, None, (f"no brief is registered for a {check.verdict} on {spec.name} — "
                            "which is the default, and for an `ok` row it is the answer")
    missing = _absent(check, brief.needs)
    if missing:
        named = ", ".join(f"`{m}`" for m in missing)
        return None, brief.audience, (
            f"the brief for a {check.verdict} on {spec.name} is written around {named}, "
            f"and this row reported {'none of them' if len(missing) > 1 else 'nothing there'} "
            "— so there is no brief rather than one with a hole in it")
    facts = {k: _spell(v) for k, v in check.extra.items()}
    facts.update(row=check.name, group=check.group, subject=check.subject,
                 detail=check.detail, verdict=check.verdict, repo=str(host.repo))
    try:
        task = brief.task.format(**facts)
        constraints = [c.format(**facts) for c in brief.constraints]
        verify = [v.format(**facts) for v in brief.verify]
    except (KeyError, IndexError) as exc:                     # noqa: BLE001 — it IS the answer
        return None, brief.audience, (
            f"the brief for a {check.verdict} on {spec.name} names {exc}, which no fact on "
            "this row supplies — a registration bug, and a missing brief is the honest "
            "reading of it")
    if brief.audience == "agent":
        verify = [*verify, _recheck(check, host)]
    return _lay_out(spec, check, brief, task, constraints, verify), brief.audience, ""


def _lay_out(spec: CheckSpec, check: Check, brief: Brief, task: str,
             constraints: list[str], verify: list[str]) -> str:
    """The brief as text, in the order somebody reads it.

    Prose is wrapped; the bullets are not, for the reason :func:`_wrap`'s docstring
    gives — a constraint or a verification is usually a command or a path, and one a
    reader cannot select in a single line is one they will retype wrong.
    """
    who = ("an agent — this is work" if brief.audience == "agent"
           else "a person — this is an escalation, not a fix")
    out = [f"qb-doctor / {spec.name} ({spec.group}) — {_GLYPHS[check.verdict]}",
           f"for: {who}",
           f"subject: {check.subject}",
           "",
           "WHAT THE ROW FOUND",
           *(f"  {line}" for line in _wrap(check.detail, 84)),
           "",
           "WHY IT MATTERS",
           *(f"  {line}" for line in _wrap(" ".join(spec.explanation.split()), 84)),
           "",
           "DO",
           *(f"  {line}" for line in _wrap(task, 84))]
    if constraints:
        out += ["", "CONSTRAINTS", *(f"  - {c}" for c in constraints)]
    if verify:
        out += ["", "DONE WHEN", *(f"  - {v}" for v in verify)]
    if check.manual:
        out += ["", "WHAT THE REPORT PRINTS FOR A PERSON (not yours to run blind)",
                f"  {check.manual}"]
    if check.extra:
        out += ["", "WHAT THIS RUN ESTABLISHED",
                *(f"  {k}: {_spell(v)}" for k, v in sorted(check.extra.items()))]
    return "\n".join(out)


#: The questions this tool asks, as opposed to the rows that answer them. Two, and
#: they are separate because a row's group decides what a reader does with a `FAIL`:
#: a broken `host` row is fixed on this machine, and a broken `landing` row is fixed
#: in a repository setting or a queue that everybody shares.
#:
#: ``landing`` arrived with #406 and is deliberately a GROUP holding one row rather
#: than a one-off. On the night #406 was filed this tool reported 9 ok / 1 unknown
#: while the merge queue held seven green pull requests with none ready, main had
#: not moved in three hours, a release tag pointed off main's history and three
#: branches were conflicting on the one file `changelog.d/` exists to keep them out
#: of. Every row was correct; not one of them was about whether work could land.
#: #407 is the rest of that group, and it should be extending a category rather
#: than retrofitting one around a single check.
GROUPS = {
    "host": "is this host wired up (#204)",
    "landing": "can work actually land (#406, #407)",
    "semantic": "do the instructions still say the right thing (#408) — not run by default",
}

#: Every check, in the order the report prints them: the three version lines and
#: what they depend on first, then the guards, then the landing group. A tuple
#: rather than a registry decorator so `--only` can validate a name against it
#: before anything runs.
CHECKS = (
    CheckSpec(
        "checkout", "host", check_checkout,
        explanation=(
            "Every row below that compares something against *this checkout* is only as "
            "current as the checkout is. `git status` says a branch is up to date with "
